HP.preview={"org":"H²S Consultants · Risk2Safe","pitch":"Human factors, process safety and HSE consulting for oil and gas, grounded in Energy Institute, IOGP and HSE guidance and 30 years of operator-side experience in the Gulf.","email":"info@risk2safe.com","phone":"+91 90031 46969","links":[["risk2safe.com","https://risk2safe.com"],["app.risk2safe.com","https://app.risk2safe.com"]]};
HP.programme({"frameworkIntro":"The pathway certifies competence in five cumulative levels. Each competency is assessed on evidence, not attendance: knowledge checks show understanding, practical assessments and capstones show that the person can do it at work.","routes":[{"role":"All site personnel and visitors with unescorted access","req":["R","-","-","-","-"]},{"role":"Field operators, technicians, maintenance crafts","req":["R","E","-","-","-"]},{"role":"Permit issuers, area authorities, control room operators","req":["R","R","O","-","-"]},{"role":"First-line supervisors and contractor supervisors","req":["R","R","-","O","-"]},{"role":"Process, maintenance and project engineers","req":["R","E","E","-","-"]},{"role":"HSE and process safety advisers","req":["R","R","R","O","O"]},{"role":"Procedure writers and maintenance planners","req":["R","R","R","-","-"]},{"role":"Superintendents and area managers","req":["R","O","-","R","-"]},{"role":"Plant managers and asset leadership team","req":["R","-","-","R","-"]},{"role":"Lead incident investigators","req":["R","R","R","E","E"]},{"role":"Human performance leads, champions and pathway facilitators","req":["R","R","R","R","R"]}],"proficiency":[["Aware (HP-1)","Recognises human performance concepts and error-likely situations; knows when to stop and ask."],["Practitioner (HP-2)","Uses the human performance tools reliably in own work and coaches peers in them."],["Analyst (HP-3)","Analyses safety-critical tasks and designs, identifies credible failures and specifies effective controls."],["Leader (HP-4)","Shapes the conditions for reliable performance, responds fairly to failure and learns from normal work."],["Master (HP-5)","Designs, runs and evaluates a human performance programme; leads systemic investigations; qualifies others."]],"references":["Energy Institute. Human factors briefing notes (series). London: EI.","Energy Institute. Guidance on human factors safety critical task analysis, 2nd edition. London: EI, 2020.","Energy Institute and CIEHF. Human factors tools to support situation awareness. London: EI.","Health and Safety Executive. HSG48 Reducing error and influencing behaviour. HSE Books, 1999.","Health and Safety Executive. Human factors: key topics (core topics for major hazard sites). HSE.","Health and Safety Executive. Contract research report CRR 348, Development of a method for assessing the safety implications of staffing levels. HSE Books, 2001.","IOGP. Report 454, Human factors engineering in projects. International Association of Oil & Gas Producers.","IOGP. Report 621, Demystifying human factors: building confidence in human factors investigation. International Association of Oil & Gas Producers, 2018.","CCPS. Guidelines for preventing human error in process safety. New York: AIChE/Wiley.","CCPS. Human factors methods for improving performance in the process industries. New York: AIChE/Wiley, 2007.","API RP 755. Fatigue risk management systems for personnel in the refining and petrochemical industries. American Petroleum Institute.","EEMUA 191. Alarm systems: a guide to design, management and procurement. Engineering Equipment and Materials Users Association.","ISO 11064 (all parts). Ergonomic design of control centres.","ISO 6385. Ergonomics principles in the design of work systems.","US Department of Energy. DOE-HDBK-1028-2009 Human performance improvement handbook.","Reason, J. Human error. Cambridge University Press, 1990.","Reason, J. Managing the risks of organizational accidents. Ashgate, 1997.","Rasmussen, J. Risk management in a dynamic society: a modelling problem. Safety Science 27, 1997.","Endsley, M. R. Toward a theory of situation awareness in dynamic systems. Human Factors 37(1), 1995.","Flin, R., O'Connor, P. and Crichton, M. Safety at the sharp end: a guide to non-technical skills. Ashgate, 2008.","Williams, J. C. HEART: a proposed method for assessing and reducing human error. 9th Advances in Reliability Technology Symposium, 1986.","Kirwan, B. A guide to practical human reliability assessment. Taylor & Francis, 1994.","Dekker, S. The field guide to understanding human error. CRC Press.","Dekker, S. Just culture: restoring trust and accountability in your organization. CRC Press.","Marx, D. Patient safety and the just culture: a primer for health care executives. 2001.","Conklin, T. Pre-accident investigations: an introduction to organizational safety. Ashgate, 2012.","Hollnagel, E. Safety-I and Safety-II: the past and future of safety management. Ashgate, 2014.","Hollnagel, E. The ETTO principle: efficiency-thoroughness trade-off. Ashgate, 2009.","Hollnagel, E. FRAM: the functional resonance analysis method. Ashgate, 2012.","Leveson, N. Engineering a safer world. MIT Press, 2011.","Weick, K. and Sutcliffe, K. Managing the unexpected. Jossey-Bass.","Vaughan, D. The Challenger launch decision. University of Chicago Press, 1996.","Westrum, R. A typology of organisational cultures. Quality and Safety in Health Care 13 (suppl. 2), 2004.","Hudson, P. Implementing a safety culture in a major multinational. Safety Science 45, 2007.","Shappell, S. and Wiegmann, D. The human factors analysis and classification system (HFACS). FAA, 2000.","Edmondson, A. Psychological safety and learning behavior in work teams. Administrative Science Quarterly 44, 1999.","US Chemical Safety Board. Investigation report: refinery explosion and fire, BP Texas City, 2007.","Hopkins, A. Lessons from Longford: the Esso gas plant explosion. CCH, 2000.","Kirkpatrick, D. and Kirkpatrick, J. Evaluating training programs: the four levels. Berrett-Koehler."],"glossary":[["Active failure","An unsafe act by someone at the sharp end whose effect is immediate (Reason)."],["Lapse","A memory failure: forgetting a step, losing place in a procedure or forgetting to remove a temporary bypass."],["Skill-rule-knowledge (SRK)","Rasmussen's three levels of performance: automatic skill-based, if-then rule-based and effortful knowledge-based."],["Error trap","A specific feature of the work or workplace that predictably leads people into error."],["Place-keeping","Marking off procedure steps as they are completed (for example circle-slash) so that steps are not skipped or repeated."],["Independent verification","A second person separately confirms a condition, after the act, without being influenced by the first person."],["Post-job review","A brief discussion after work about what went as planned, what surprised the crew and what should change next time."],["Non-technical skills","Cognitive and social skills such as situation awareness, decision-making, communication, teamwork, leadership and managing stress and fatigue."],["Normalisation of deviance","The gradual acceptance of a departure from standards as normal because nothing bad has happened yet (Vaughan)."],["Hierarchical task analysis (HTA)","Breaking a task into goals, sub-tasks and steps, with plans that state the order and conditions."],["Error-producing condition (EPC)","In HEART, a condition that multiplies the nominal error probability of a generic task, adjusted by the assessed proportion of affect."],["Just culture","An atmosphere of trust in which people are encouraged to report safety information, with a clear line between acceptable and unacceptable behaviour."],["Psychological safety","A shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking such as speaking up or admitting a mistake (Edmondson)."],["ETTO","The efficiency-thoroughness trade-off: people constantly balance doing things quickly against doing them thoroughly (Hollnagel)."],["Outcome bias","Judging a decision by its outcome rather than by the quality of the decision at the time."],["STAMP / CAST","Systems-Theoretic Accident Model and Processes; CAST is its accident analysis method, focusing on inadequate control and feedback (Leveson)."],["Chronic unease","A healthy wariness and scepticism about risk control, the opposite of complacency."],["Fatigue risk management system (FRMS)","A data-driven, risk-based system for managing fatigue, going beyond hours-of-work limits (API RP 755 sets out the requirements for refining and petrochemical sites)."]],"glossaryTotal":54,"toolkit":null,"toolkitTeaser":["Roll-out in four waves","Delivery standards","Facilitator qualification","Assessment and certification policy","Evaluating the pathway","Course logistics checklist"]});
HP.level({"id":"L1","n":1,"name":"Foundation","title":"Human Performance Foundations","badge":"HP-1","tagline":"Everyone on site learns why good people make errors, how to spot the traps before they bite, and which simple habits and conversations keep themselves and their crews safe.","audience":"All workforce on an operating site or project: operators, technicians, maintenance crafts, contractors and subcontractors, new starters, drivers, warehouse and administrative staff who work on or visit the plant. No supervisory role is assumed.","prereq":"None. Site induction completed (or scheduled). Participants should be able to follow a spoken and written briefing in the course language; interpreters or bilingual co-facilitators are arranged in advance for mixed-language crews.","duration":"1 day, 07:30-16:15 (approx. 7.5 h contact including assessment and practical sign-off)","format":"Classroom or site training room, 8-20 participants, one lead facilitator plus one co-facilitator for groups over 12. Highly interactive: short inputs, table-group scenario work, card sorts, role-play of tools, and a personal commitment. Designed to be delivered by a competent site trainer using this guide; no prior HF qualification required for the facilitator, but completion of HP-2 or equivalent is recommended.","outcomes":["Describe the difference between the old view (people as the problem) and the new view (people as the solution, working inside a system) and state the five human performance principles in their own words.","Recognise and give an oil and gas example of a slip, a lapse, a rule-based mistake, a knowledge-based mistake and a violation, and explain why each needs a different kind of control.","Identify at least six error precursors (error-likely conditions) in a described field task, grouped by task demands, work environment, individual capabilities and human nature.","Describe when and how to use STAR, a questioning attitude, three-way communication, place-keeping, peer checking and stop-work authority on their own work.","Explain how fatigue, heat, stress and personal wellbeing affect performance, recognise the warning signs in themselves and others, and state what to do when they or a colleague are not fit for duty.","Explain why near misses and error traps should be reported, what makes a good report, and what a just culture will and will not do with their report.","Demonstrate, in a short observed role-play, a correct three-way communication and a confident stop-when-unsure intervention."],"competencies":[{"code":"HP1.1","statement":"Explains human performance as the interaction of people, plant and process and describes the five HP principles.","evidence":"Knowledge check items (M1); contribution to M1 scenario debrief recorded by facilitator."},{"code":"HP1.2","statement":"Recognises and classifies types of human failure (slip, lapse, mistake, violation) in workplace examples.","evidence":"Knowledge check items (M2); correct classification of at least 4 of 5 events in the M2 card sort."},{"code":"HP1.3","statement":"Identifies error precursors and red-flag conditions before starting a task.","evidence":"Practical task A: identifies at least 6 precursors across at least 3 TWIN categories in an unseen task description."},{"code":"HP1.4","statement":"Applies basic personal HP tools: STAR self-check, three-way communication and stop-when-unsure.","evidence":"Practical task B: observed role-play, marked against the rubric."},{"code":"HP1.5","statement":"Recognises fatigue, heat stress and other fitness-for-duty concerns in self and others and knows the correct response.","evidence":"Knowledge check items (M5); M5 scenario answers."},{"code":"HP1.6","statement":"Reports near misses, unsafe conditions and error traps in a clear, factual, blame-free way and understands the just culture response.","evidence":"Practical task C: completes a near-miss report from a scenario to the rubric standard; 30-day workplace follow-up report."}],"schedule":[{"block":"07:30-07:45","items":"Arrival, welcome, site safety brief, course objectives and ground rules (psychological safety in the room)."},{"block":"07:45-08:55","items":"M1 Why human performance matters: people, plant, process; old and new view; five principles. Scenario: The flange that was opened twice."},{"block":"08:55-10:05","items":"M2 How errors happen: System 1 and 2; SRK; slips, lapses, mistakes, violations. Scenario: A night on the export pumps."},{"block":"10:05-10:20","items":"Break (refreshments)."},{"block":"10:20-11:30","items":"M3 Error traps and error-likely situations: PSFs, TWIN, red flags, critical steps. Scenario: Sampling a sour separator on a busy afternoon."},{"block":"11:30-12:15","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"12:15-13:20","items":"M4 Your personal HP toolkit: STAR, questioning attitude, stop when unsure, three-way communication, place-keeping, peer check. Scenario and role-play: Draining the condensate pot (Practical task B observed here)."},{"block":"13:20-14:15","items":"M5 Fatigue, heat, stress and fitness for duty: API RP 755 awareness, heat illness, IMSAFE. Scenario: Day 19 of the turnaround."},{"block":"14:15-14:25","items":"Short break."},{"block":"14:25-15:25","items":"M6 Speaking up and reporting: near misses, CUS, psychological safety, just culture, what happens to your report. Scenario: The welding habitat by the wellhead (Practical task C completed here)."},{"block":"15:25-16:05","items":"Assessment: knowledge check (20 MCQs, 20 min) and Practical task A precursor hunt (10 min); personal commitment card and 30-day follow-up briefing (10 min)."},{"block":"16:05-16:15","items":"Course evaluation, feedback on results, close."}],"modules":[{"id":"L1M1","n":1,"t":"Why human performance matters","mins":70,"obj":"Explain human performance as the interaction of people, plant and process, contrast the old and new views of error, and state the five principles that the rest of the course builds on.","tools":["Old view / new view comparison card","Five HP principles pocket card"],"learn":"\n<h2>What we mean by human performance</h2>\n<p>Every barrier on a hydrocarbon facility depends on people at some point. Someone designed the relief system, someone wrote the isolation procedure, someone tested the shutdown valve last month, someone is reading the level on the separator now, and someone will sign the permit that lets work start tomorrow. <b>Human performance</b> is the study and management of how people actually do that work, so that the work goes right more often and, when something does go wrong, the result is small and recoverable.</p>\n<p>Human performance is not a separate safety programme. It sits across three things that are always present on a site:</p>\n<div class=\"grid3\">\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>People</h4><p>Their skills, experience, alertness, health, expectations and the way they communicate. Includes contractors, visitors and the control room as much as the field crew.</p></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Plant</h4><p>Equipment, valves, tags, displays, alarms, access, lighting, tools and PPE. Whether the plant makes the right action easy and the wrong action hard.</p></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Process</h4><p>Procedures, permits, isolations, planning, shift patterns, handovers, supervision and how the organisation reacts to good and bad news.</p></div></div>\n<p>A task goes well when these three fit together. It goes badly when one of them sets a trap: two identical drain and bypass valves side by side, a permit that does not say exactly which flanges to break, an operator on the 13th night shift in a row. The person who springs the trap is usually the last link in a chain, not the cause of it.</p>\n\n<h2>Old view and new view</h2>\n<p>For much of the last century, incidents were explained by finding the person who made the mistake. That approach is now called the <b>old view</b> (or the person approach). The <b>new view</b> (the systems approach) treats the error as a symptom of conditions in the workplace, and asks what made the action seem sensible to the person at the time.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Old view says</th><th>New view says</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Human error is the cause of the incident.</td><td>Human error is a consequence of conditions in the system. It is where the investigation starts, not where it ends.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>People are the problem; take them out or control them.</td><td>People are the main source of safety: they adapt, notice and recover problems every shift.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fix it with retraining, warnings, discipline and \"be more careful\".</td><td>Fix it by changing the design, the procedure, the workload or the information so that the next person cannot fall into the same trap.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Judge the action by its outcome, with hindsight.</td><td>Understand the action from inside the situation: what the person could see, knew and expected at that moment.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Safety is the absence of incidents.</td><td>Safety is the presence of capacity: barriers, margins, and people able to detect and recover from problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><h4>Hindsight is not insight</h4><p>After an event we know the outcome, so the warning signs look obvious. The person inside the event did not know the outcome. Their attention was on what looked important then. Before asking \"why didn't they see it?\" ask \"what were they looking at, and why did that make sense?\"</p></div>\n\n<h2>The five principles</h2>\n<p>The human performance approach used across nuclear, aviation and now oil and gas rests on five principles, set out in the US Department of Energy Human Performance Improvement Handbook (DOE-HDBK-1028-2009) and echoed in Human and Organisational Performance (HOP). We use them all day.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>People are fallible, and even the best people make mistakes.</b> Error is normal. Experience reduces some errors and creates others (habits, overconfidence). Plan for error rather than hope it will not happen.</li>\n<li><b>Error-likely situations are predictable, manageable and preventable.</b> The conditions that provoke error (time pressure, first-time tasks, confusing displays, interruptions, fatigue) can be spotted before the job starts. Module 3 is about finding them.</li>\n<li><b>Individual behaviour is influenced by organisational processes and values.</b> People do what the system rewards and makes easy. If the schedule always beats the procedure, the procedure will lose.</li>\n<li><b>People achieve high levels of performance largely because of the encouragement and reinforcement they receive from leaders, peers and subordinates.</b> Behaviour that is noticed and reinforced is repeated: good and bad.</li>\n<li><b>Events can be avoided through an understanding of the reasons mistakes occur and application of the lessons learned from past events.</b> Learning, including from near misses and normal work, is how the system improves.</li></ol>\n<p>The HOP version phrases these as: error is normal; blame fixes nothing; learning and improving is vital; context drives behaviour; and <b>how leaders respond to failure matters</b>. That last point is critical. If a crew sees a colleague disciplined for reporting a mistake, the next mistake will not be reported, and the organisation loses its warning signals.</p>\n<div class=\"rev\"><button type=\"button\">Does the new view mean nobody is ever accountable?</button><div class=\"body\"><p>No. The new view separates <b>blame</b> (looking back, punishing the person) from <b>accountability</b> (looking forward, everyone owning their part in making the system safer). Reckless disregard for safety is still dealt with. Module 6 covers just culture, which draws that line fairly.</p></div></div>\n\n<h2>Why it matters in oil and gas</h2>\n<p>Our industry handles large inventories of flammable and toxic material. A single wrong action at the wrong moment can bypass several engineered barriers. Public investigations of major accidents show the same pattern repeatedly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Piper Alpha, North Sea, 1988 (167 killed).</b> A condensate pump was started on night shift while its pressure safety valve had been removed for maintenance on day shift. The permit-to-work and shift handover did not carry that information to the people who needed it.</li>\n<li><b>Longford, Australia, 1998 (2 killed).</b> After a loss of lean oil flow, metal became extremely cold. Operators restarting flow did not know of the brittle fracture hazard because training and procedures had not given them that knowledge. The Royal Commission did not accept operator error as the explanation.</li>\n<li><b>Texas City, USA, 2005 (15 killed, 180 injured).</b> A raffinate splitter tower was overfilled during startup. The CSB found, among many issues, a level transmitter that gave misleading readings, a poor shift handover, and a board operator who had worked 12-hour shifts for 29 consecutive days.</li></ul>\n<p>In each case individuals made errors. In each case the conditions that made those errors likely had been building for months or years, and the organisation had chances to see them.</p>\n\n<h2>Your role at Foundation level</h2>\n<p>This course does not ask you to become a human factors specialist. It asks you to do four things: understand why errors happen; notice error traps before they catch you or a colleague; use a small set of simple habits that catch errors before they matter; and speak up and report so that the organisation can fix the traps for everyone.</p>\n<h3>Sort it</h3><p>Decide whether each response to an event reflects the old view or the new view.</p>\n<div class=\"sortq\" data-sort='[[\"Ask what made the action seem reasonable at the time\",\"New view\"],[\"Add a final written warning to the technician file\",\"Old view\"],[\"Close the report as operator carelessness\",\"Old view\"],[\"Move the tag so it faces the walkway and re-label the valve pair\",\"New view\"],[\"Remind everyone in the toolbox talk to pay more attention\",\"Old view\"],[\"Thank the person who reported the near miss and share the learning\",\"New view\"]]' data-opts='[\"Old view\",\"New view\"]'></div>\n<p class=\"src\">Based on DOE-HDBK-1028-2009 Human Performance Improvement Handbook Vol. 1 (principles of human performance); Conklin, Pre-Accident Investigations and the HOP principles; Dekker, The Field Guide to Understanding Human Error (old and new view); Reason (1997) Managing the Risks of Organizational Accidents; Energy Institute human factors briefing notes; public reports: Cullen Inquiry (Piper Alpha), Longford Royal Commission, US CSB Texas City report.</p>","activity":{"title":"The flange that was opened twice","time":"35 minutes","scn":"<p><span class=\"tag\">Onshore gas gathering station · Gulf region</span><span class=\"tag\">Day shift, turnaround week 2 · 41 °C</span></p>\n<p>During a turnaround at a gas gathering station, a contractor mechanical crew was working on a permit to replace a spectacle blind gasket on the inlet line to a slug catcher. The line had been isolated, drained and purged by operations the previous day. The isolation certificate listed the valves locked closed and the drain points; it referred to the flange by a P&amp;ID line number but not by a physical tag.</p>\n<p>At 10:30 the crew was told to pause because a crane lift was taking place overhead (a SIMOPS conflict). They returned at 13:15 after lunch and prayer. Meanwhile the area operator had changed at 12:00; the outgoing operator told the incoming operator that \"the slug catcher job is done apart from re-bolting\". The crew foreman went back to what he believed was the same flange. It was the adjacent flange on a parallel line that looked identical and had not been isolated. As the second stud was loosened, gas began to weep from the joint. A crew member noticed the hiss, the crew backed off, re-tightened the stud and called the control room. Personal H2S monitors did not alarm; the fixed detector nearby registered a brief low %LEL reading. No one was hurt.</p>\n<p>At the morning meeting the turnaround manager says: \"The foreman has 20 years' experience. He knows to check the tag. I want him off site and the crew retrained.\"</p>","qs":[{"q":"List the people, plant and process conditions that set up this event.","hint":"At least two in each category. Think about the flange identification, the interruption, the handover, the permit wording and the turnaround context."},{"q":"Which of the five HP principles are most clearly illustrated? Explain two of them using details from the case.","hint":"Principle 1 (even an experienced foreman is fallible); principle 2 (the interruption and identical flanges were predictable); principle 3 (permit and handover processes); principle 5 (learning)."},{"q":"What does the turnaround manager's response tell the workforce, and what is the likely effect on reporting?","hint":"Link to how leaders respond to failure, and to the crew member who recovered the situation."},{"q":"Propose four actions that would make it harder for the next crew to open the wrong flange.","hint":"Prefer physical and design changes, then information, then checks. Who owns each?"}]},"quiz":[{"q":"Which statement best reflects the new view of human error?","opts":["Most incidents are caused by careless workers who need closer supervision.","Human error is a symptom of conditions in the system and is the starting point of an investigation.","Human error can be eliminated with enough training and discipline.","Errors only happen to inexperienced or poorly motivated people."],"a":1,"why":"The new view treats error as a consequence of the conditions people work in. The investigation asks what made the action seem reasonable and what in the system should change."},{"q":"According to the DOE human performance principles, error-likely situations are:","opts":["Random and therefore impossible to prepare for.","Only identifiable after an incident investigation.","Predictable, manageable and preventable.","The responsibility of the safety department alone."],"a":2,"why":"Principle 2: the conditions that provoke error (time pressure, first-time tasks, interruptions, confusing equipment) can be recognised in advance and managed."},{"q":"A crew member reports that he nearly opened the wrong valve. Which leadership response best supports future reporting?","opts":["Thank him, find out what made the wrong valve look right, and fix the condition.","Record a verbal warning but take no further action.","Tell him to be more careful and close the report.","Ask the safety department to investigate who else has made the same mistake."],"a":0,"why":"How leaders respond to failure determines whether the next problem is reported. Thanking, learning and fixing the condition reinforces reporting."},{"q":"Which of these is the best example of the 'process' element of human performance?","opts":["The colour and position of two drain valves.","An operator's level of fatigue.","The wording of the isolation certificate and the shift handover practice.","The noise level near a compressor."],"a":2,"why":"Process covers procedures, permits, planning, handover and organisational arrangements. Valve layout and noise are plant/environment; fatigue is a people factor."},{"q":"Why is hindsight dangerous when reviewing what someone did?","opts":["It makes investigations take longer.","Knowing the outcome makes the warning signs look more obvious than they were to the person at the time.","It always blames management instead of the worker.","It is not dangerous; hindsight shows exactly what the person should have done."],"a":1,"why":"Hindsight bias makes the correct path look obvious after the event. The person inside the situation did not know the outcome and was paying attention to other cues."},{"q":"In the Piper Alpha disaster (1988), which human performance issue is widely cited from the public inquiry?","opts":["A crane operator dropped a load onto the gas compression module.","Information about a removed pressure safety valve did not reach the night shift through the permit and handover system.","Operators deliberately disabled the fire and gas system to increase production.","A control room operator deliberately silenced a gas alarm to stop nuisance alarms."],"a":1,"why":"The Cullen Inquiry found that the night shift started a condensate pump whose PSV had been removed under a permit; the permit-to-work and shift handover failed to communicate this."}],"nfac":6},{"id":"L1M2","n":2,"t":"How errors happen","mins":70,"obj":"Recognise slips, lapses, mistakes and violations, explain them using the skill-rule-knowledge model and fast and slow thinking, and describe why each type needs a different control.","tools":["Human failure types card (slip, lapse, mistake, violation)","SRK quick reference"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"A night on the export pumps","exTime":"40 minutes"},{"id":"L1M3","n":3,"t":"Error traps and error-likely situations","mins":70,"obj":"Identify performance shaping factors and error precursors in a field task using the TWIN categories, and recognise the red-flag conditions that should make anyone pause before starting or continuing work.","tools":["TWIN error precursor checklist","Red-flag conditions card"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"Sampling a sour separator on a busy afternoon","exTime":"30 minutes"},{"id":"L1M4","n":4,"t":"Your personal human performance toolkit","mins":65,"obj":"Describe the purpose of six basic error-reduction tools (STAR, questioning attitude, stop when unsure, three-way communication, place-keeping and peer checking) and demonstrate STAR and three-way communication in a simple field task.","tools":["STAR self-check card","Three-way communication radio card","Place-keeping marking convention","Stop-work authority script"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"Draining the condensate pot","exTime":"30 minutes"},{"id":"L1M5","n":5,"t":"Fatigue, heat, stress and fitness for duty","mins":55,"obj":"Explain how fatigue, heat, stress and wellbeing affect performance, recognise the warning signs in themselves and colleagues, and describe the correct fitness-for-duty response.","tools":["IMSAFE fitness-for-duty self-check","Heat illness recognition and response card","Fatigue warning signs card"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1200,"exTitle":"Day 19 of the turnaround","exTime":"25 minutes"},{"id":"L1M6","n":6,"t":"Speaking up and reporting","mins":60,"obj":"Explain why near misses and error traps must be reported, write a clear, factual report, speak up using a simple structure, and describe how a just culture treats the person who reports.","tools":["Near-miss and error trap report template","CUS speaking-up card","Just culture response guide (console, coach, sanction)"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"The welding habitat by the wellhead","exTime":"30 minutes"}],"assessment":{"summary":"Two parts. (1) A closed-book knowledge check of 20 multiple-choice questions drawn from the six module question banks (minimum three per module), taken at the end of the day on paper or in the app. (2) A practical assessment of three short tasks observed and marked by the facilitator during the day (tasks B and C) and at the end (task A), against the rubric below. A 30-day workplace follow-up, confirmed by the participant's supervisor, completes the HP-1 record. Reasonable adjustments (oral assessment, interpreter, extra time) are offered for participants with limited literacy in the course language; the standard assessed is understanding, not writing ability.","pass":"80% or more on the knowledge check (16 of 20) AND 'Competent' on all three practical tasks. One re-sit of the knowledge check and one repeat of any practical task is allowed on the day after feedback. The 30-day workplace follow-up is required for the certificate to be issued.","practical":null},"capstone":null});
HP.level({"id":"L2","n":2,"name":"Practitioner","title":"Human Performance Tools in Practice","badge":"HP-2","tagline":"Turn human performance principles into the everyday tools that catch errors before they reach the plant: briefs, self-checks, verification, clear communication, a questioning attitude and situation awareness at the workface.","audience":"Frontline operators (field and control room), maintenance technicians and crews, permit holders and performing authorities, first-line supervisors, and contractor supervisors working on operating plant, during turnarounds or under SIMOPS.","prereq":"HP-1 Human Performance Foundations (or equivalent), plus current site permit-to-work and isolation awareness. Supervisors attending Module 7 should hold, or be about to take up, a supervisory role.","duration":"2 days (approximately 14 h contact including assessment)","format":"Face-to-face, maximum 16 participants in mixed crews of 4. About 60% practice: role-play briefs and handovers, table-top procedure walk-throughs, verification drills with tagged mock-ups, radio exercises and field-observation simulations. Delivered in English with bilingual facilitation available; all job aids are pocket cards participants keep.","outcomes":["Lead a pre-job brief that identifies the critical steps of a real task, what could go wrong, the worst credible outcome and agreed if-then contingencies, and run a post-job review that produces at least one actionable learning.","Demonstrate STAR self-checking at critical steps and circle-slash place-keeping on a continuous-use procedure, including correct recovery after an interruption.","Select and perform the right verification practice (peer check, concurrent verification or independent verification) for a given task, and explain what makes a verification genuinely independent.","Use three-way communication, the phonetic alphabet and digit-by-digit read-back for safety-critical instructions, and deliver a structured shift and permit handover combining written, verbal and walk-down elements.","Apply a questioning attitude, call a time-out when unsure, recognise signs of drift and normalisation of deviance, and use flagging to prevent wrong-unit, wrong-train and wrong-component errors.","Use Endsley's three levels to check their own and their crew's situation awareness, recognise SA red flags, and apply practical techniques to manage interruptions, distraction and expectation bias.","(Supervisors) Coach crews in the use of HP tools in the field, carry out a task observation that identifies error-likely situations, and hold a field learning conversation without blame."],"competencies":[{"code":"HP2.1","statement":"Prepares for and closes out work using pre-job briefs focused on critical steps and post-job reviews that capture learning.","evidence":"Observed role-play brief scored against the brief checklist, plus one workplace brief and post-job review signed off by the supervisor within 30 days."},{"code":"HP2.2","statement":"Uses STAR self-checking and place-keeping correctly, and follows the required procedure use level.","evidence":"Practical station: performs a continuous-use procedure with an injected interruption; place-keeping marks and recovery assessed."},{"code":"HP2.3","statement":"Applies peer check, concurrent verification and independent verification appropriately and without rubber-stamping.","evidence":"Practical station: verifies a tagged isolation mock-up with seeded errors; selection of verification type explained."},{"code":"HP2.4","statement":"Communicates safety-critical information accurately using three-way communication, phonetic alphabet and structured handover.","evidence":"Radio exercise and handover role-play scored on the communication rubric; written handover log reviewed."},{"code":"HP2.5","statement":"Challenges uncertainty, stops when unsure and uses flagging to protect against wrong-component errors.","evidence":"Scenario response in the practical assessment and knowledge check items; workplace example of a time-out or flagging recorded in the logbook."},{"code":"HP2.6","statement":"Maintains situation awareness at the workface and manages interruptions and distraction.","evidence":"SA scenario debrief and knowledge check; self-assessment against the SA red-flag card."},{"code":"HP2.7","statement":"(Supervisors) Coaches HP tool use, observes tasks for error-likely situations and leads blame-free field learning conversations.","evidence":"Coaching role-play scored against the coaching rubric, plus two workplace task observations with learning conversation notes reviewed by the HP lead."}],"schedule":[{"block":"Day 1 07:30-07:50","items":"Welcome, course safety brief, HP-1 recap: error types, local rationality and the systems view. Crews formed for the two days."},{"block":"Day 1 07:50-09:30","items":"M1 Pre-job briefs and post-job reviews: critical steps, SAFER, if-then contingencies; brief role-play at a mock job site."},{"block":"Day 1 09:30-09:45","items":"Break"},{"block":"Day 1 09:45-11:20","items":"M2 Self-checking (STAR), place-keeping and procedure use: sour sampling scenario; circle-slash practical with injected interruption."},{"block":"Day 1 11:20-12:05","items":"Lunch and prayer"},{"block":"Day 1 12:05-13:35","items":"M3 Verification practices: peer check, concurrent and independent verification; spade board drill run twice."},{"block":"Day 1 13:35-13:50","items":"Break"},{"block":"Day 1 13:50-15:40","items":"M4 Safety-critical communication and handover: radio exercise with noise and ear defenders; SRU handover role-play; permit handover."},{"block":"Day 1 15:40-16:00","items":"Day 1 post-job review: crews run the five-question review on the day itself and post one learning each."},{"block":"Day 2 07:30-07:45","items":"Recap and quick radio drill with the phonetic alphabet."},{"block":"Day 2 07:45-09:10","items":"M5 Questioning attitude, stop when unsure, drift and flagging: wrong-train scenario; drift sticky-note exercise."},{"block":"Day 2 09:10-09:25","items":"Break"},{"block":"Day 2 09:25-11:00","items":"M6 Situation awareness at the workface: SIMOPS freeze-frame exercise; interruption management."},{"block":"Day 2 11:00-11:45","items":"Lunch and prayer"},{"block":"Day 2 11:45-13:20","items":"M7 Supervisor field skills: TWIN task observation, coaching role-play in threes (assessed as Station E for supervisors), learning conversations. Frontline participants play crew and give feedback."},{"block":"Day 2 13:20-13:30","items":"Break and station briefing"},{"block":"Day 2 13:30-15:00","items":"Practical assessment: Stations A to D in rotation, about 20 minutes each, with feedback after each. (Station E for supervisors is scored during the M7 coaching role-play.)"},{"block":"Day 2 15:00-15:30","items":"Knowledge check: 30 questions, closed book."},{"block":"Day 2 15:30-16:00","items":"Personal application plan, workplace logbook briefing, course post-job review and close."}],"modules":[{"id":"L2M1","n":1,"t":"Pre-job briefs and post-job reviews","mins":100,"obj":"Lead a pre-job brief built around critical steps, credible failure and if-then contingencies, and close the job with a post-job review that feeds learning back into the next job.","tools":["SAFER pre-job brief card","Critical step identification questions","If-then contingency worksheet","Five-question post-job review card"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1300,"exTitle":"Removing a control valve on sour condensate service","exTime":"45 minutes (20 group work, 15 role-play, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L2M2","n":2,"t":"Self-checking (STAR), place-keeping and procedure use","mins":95,"obj":"Demonstrate STAR self-checking at critical steps, use circle-slash place-keeping on a continuous-use procedure, and apply the correct procedure use level and adherence rules.","tools":["STAR self-check card","Circle-slash place-keeping guide","Procedure use level decision aid","Interruption recovery checklist"],"learn":"\n<h2>Why skilled people need a self-check</h2>\n<p>Slips and lapses happen to experienced people doing familiar work. When a task is routine, the brain runs it on autopilot (skill-based behaviour, in Rasmussen's terms), and attention drifts to the next job, the radio, the heat or the end of the shift. Most of the time this is efficient and safe. At a critical step it is not. Self-checking is a deliberate, short switch from autopilot to conscious attention at the moment it matters most.</p>\n<h2>STAR: Stop, Think, Act, Review</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What you do</th><th>Example: opening a sour drain valve</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Stop</b></td><td>Pause before the action. Remove distractions. Put the radio call on hold.</td><td>Hand off the valve. Tell the control room \"Wait one\" if they call.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Think</b></td><td>Confirm the right component, the right action and the expected result. Ask: what will I see if this goes right, and what will I do if it does not?</td><td>\"This is Two-One-Hotel-Victor-Zero-Four-Seven, the low-point drain on Train B. I expect liquid to the closed drain and the sight glass to show flow. If I get gas at the vent or my monitor alarms, I close and walk upwind.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Act</b></td><td>Touch the label, then the component. Perform the action once, deliberately.</td><td>Touch the tag, read it aloud, then open the valve slowly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Review</b></td><td>Check that the result is what you expected. If not, stop and put the plant in a safe state.</td><td>Watch the sight glass and the gauge. Confirm flow and falling pressure before moving on.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two practical techniques strengthen the Act and Review steps. <b>Touch-and-verify</b>: physically touch the equipment label, then the component, before acting. <b>Point and call</b>: point at the component and say its identity and the intended action aloud. Pointing and calling is long established on Japanese railways and has been adopted on many process plants because it forces the eyes, hands and voice to agree.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><h4>What defeats a self-check</h4><p>Self-checking is weakest exactly where it is most needed: under time pressure, when fatigued, when the component looks like its neighbour, and when you expect to see something. You can look straight at a tag and read what you expect, not what is written. That is why STAR is backed by labelling, flagging and, for the highest-consequence steps, a second person.</p></div>\n<h2>Procedure use levels</h2>\n<p>Not every procedure needs to be read line by line in the field. Many organisations, following DOE and nuclear practice, assign each procedure one of three use levels based on the consequence of error, the complexity of the task and how often it is done.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use level</th><th>What it requires</th><th>Typical oil and gas examples</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Continuous use</b></td><td>The procedure is at the work location and open. Each step is read before it is performed and marked as complete before moving on. Place-keeping is required.</td><td>Plant start-up after a turnaround; fired heater burner light-off; ESD and trip function testing; sour sampling with a sample cylinder; first-time or infrequent tasks; any procedure with several critical steps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reference use</b></td><td>The procedure is available at the work location. It is consulted at defined points or for the sections the user is less familiar with, and critical sections are followed step by step.</td><td>Routine pump changeovers by an experienced operator; filter changeovers; routine chemical injection rate changes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Information use</b></td><td>The task may be done from memory. The document is available for information and training.</td><td>General operating guidelines; housekeeping routines; low-consequence, frequent tasks.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The use level should be printed on the front page of the procedure. If it is not, ask the procedure owner, and treat any task with a critical step as continuous use until told otherwise.</p>\n<h2>Procedure adherence</h2>\n<p>Adherence means doing the task as the procedure describes, in the sequence written. Procedures are imperfect; HSG48 and the Energy Institute both note that unworkable procedures are a major cause of violations. The answer is not to improvise, and not to follow a wrong procedure blindly. The answer is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>If a step cannot be done as written, or the plant does not respond as the procedure expects, <b>stop</b>.</li>\n<li>Put the plant and the work in a safe, stable condition.</li>\n<li>Contact the supervisor or the control room.</li>\n<li>Get the procedure changed through the site's temporary change or management of change process before continuing.</li>\n<li>Report the problem so the procedure is fixed permanently.</li></ol>\n<p>A procedure that is regularly worked around is a signal to the organisation, not just a failing of the people working around it.</p>\n<h2>Place-keeping</h2>\n<p>Place-keeping is the physical marking of a procedure so that you always know which steps are complete, which is in progress and which come next. It protects against the most common lapse in procedural work: losing your place after an interruption, then skipping or repeating a step. The method most widely taught is <b>circle-slash</b>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Before starting a step, read it and <b>circle</b> the step number.</li>\n<li>Perform the step, self-checking as required.</li>\n<li>When the step is complete and the result confirmed, draw a <b>slash</b> through the circle.</li>\n<li>For a step that does not apply, mark it \"N/A\" with your initials, following the site's rules for who may authorise N/A.</li>\n<li>Where the procedure requires sign-off or a recorded value, record it at the time, not at the end.</li></ol>\n<p>A circle without a slash tells you, or the person who takes over from you, that a step was started but not confirmed. That is exactly the information you need after an interruption.</p>\n<h3>Recovering from an interruption</h3>\n<p>When interrupted during a continuous-use procedure: mark your place before you leave (a circle on the current step); on return, go back to the <b>last fully completed step</b>, re-read it, and confirm in the field that the plant is in the state the procedure expects; then continue. Never resume from memory. If someone else has to take over, hand over the marked-up copy in person and walk through it.</p>\n<div class=\"rev\"><button type=\"button\">Is a tick at the end of the job place-keeping?</button><div class=\"body\"><p>No. Ticking all the steps after the job is complete is record-keeping, and a poor one, because it records what you believe you did. Place-keeping is done step by step in real time. The order of the marks matters: circle before, slash after.</p></div></div>\n<h3>Sort it</h3><p>Decide which procedure use level fits each task best.</p>\n<div class=\"sortq\" data-sort='[[\"First start-up of a gas turbine compressor after a major overhaul\",\"Continuous use\"],[\"Taking an H2S sour gas sample using a sample cylinder\",\"Continuous use\"],[\"Routine changeover of duty and standby cooling water pumps by an experienced operator\",\"Reference use\"],[\"Changing over a duplex lube oil filter\",\"Reference use\"],[\"General housekeeping guidance for the control building\",\"Information use\"]]' data-opts='[\"Continuous use\",\"Reference use\",\"Information use\"]'></div>\n<p class=\"src\">Based on DOE-HDBK-1028-2009 (self-checking, procedure use and adherence, place-keeping), INPO human performance tools, HSE HSG48 (procedures and violations), Energy Institute human factors briefing notes on procedures, and Rasmussen's skill-rule-knowledge framework.</p>","activity":{"title":"The sour gas sample that vented","time":"40 minutes (15 group work, 15 practical demonstration, 10 debrief)","scn":"<p><span class=\"tag\">Sour gas plant · inlet facilities · Gulf region</span><span class=\"tag\">Night shift · 02:10 · humid, 31 °C</span></p>\n<p>The laboratory asked for an inlet gas sample from the slug catcher outlet for H₂S and CO₂ analysis. The sampling procedure is a 14-step continuous-use procedure on two pages. It includes connecting the sample cylinder, purging through the cylinder to the flare via a purge valve, closing the cylinder valves in sequence, closing the purge valve, and depressurising the sample tubing to flare before disconnecting.</p>\n<p>The field operator is experienced and has taken this sample many times. He carries the procedure folded in his coverall pocket and does not take it out; he is wearing breathing apparatus as the procedure requires. At step 8 the control room calls him on the radio to support a permit sign-off at the compressor house 200 metres away. He leaves the sample point with the cylinder connected and the purge valve open to flare. Twenty minutes later he returns and resumes from memory at step 10, closing the cylinder outlet valve. Believing he had already closed the purge valve before he left, he goes straight to the second half of step 11 and opens vent valve V-3 to depressurise the tubing. With the purge valve still open, the sample point is now lined up to V-3; sour gas vents locally, the fixed H₂S detector next to the sample point goes into high alarm and the control room initiates the area evacuation. Nobody is hurt.</p>\n<p>The procedure's step 11 combines two actions in one sentence: \"Close purge valve and depressurise tubing to flare via vent valve V-3.\" The procedure says \"to flare\" but V-3 on the panel actually vents to atmosphere through a short stack; the flare connection was removed in a modification three years ago.</p>","qs":[{"q":"1. What use level should this procedure have, and was it applied?","hint":"Consider consequence of error, H₂S, frequency, and how the operator actually used the document."},{"q":"2. Exactly where would circle-slash place-keeping have changed the outcome?","hint":"Walk through steps 8 to 11 and say what marks would have been on the page when he left and when he returned."},{"q":"3. At which steps should STAR have been applied, and what would 'Think' have sounded like at step 11?","hint":"Name the expected result and the if-then for each critical step."},{"q":"4. What system factors made this event likely?","hint":"Look at the procedure design, the modification, the interruption and the radio call."},{"q":"5. Practical: using the training procedure provided, demonstrate STAR and circle-slash, including an injected interruption.","hint":"The observer checks the marks, the recovery to the last completed step, and touch-and-verify."}]},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the R in STAR stand for, and what does it require?","opts":["Repeat: do the action twice to be sure","Report: tell the control room after every step","Review: confirm the result is what you expected, and stop if it is not","Record: write the step number on the permit"],"a":2,"why":"Review is the check that the action produced the expected result. If it did not, the person stops and puts the plant in a safe state."},{"q":"In circle-slash place-keeping, when is the slash drawn?","opts":["When the step has been performed and its result confirmed","Before reading the step","At the end of the whole procedure","When the supervisor signs the procedure"],"a":0,"why":"The circle is drawn before a step is performed and the slash only once it is complete and confirmed. The order shows exactly where you are."},{"q":"An operator returns to a continuous-use procedure after an interruption. What should they do?","opts":["Carry on from the step they remember being on","Start the whole procedure again from step 1","Ask the control room which step they were on","Go back to the last fully completed step, re-read it and confirm the plant state in the field before continuing"],"a":3,"why":"Resuming from memory is the classic cause of skipped steps. Returning to the last completed step and verifying the field condition re-establishes the correct place."},{"q":"A step in a procedure cannot be done as written because the valve has been removed. What is the correct response?","opts":["Skip the step and note it at the end","Stop, make the situation safe, contact the supervisor and get the procedure changed through the proper process before continuing","Do the nearest equivalent action and continue","Ask a colleague what they normally do"],"a":1,"why":"Adherence does not mean following a wrong procedure or improvising. Stop, make safe, and get the procedure changed through the site's temporary change or MOC route."},{"q":"Which task is most likely to require continuous use?","opts":["Daily housekeeping of the control building","Changing over a duplex filter on a familiar lube oil system","Reading the weekly operations bulletin","Fired heater burner light-off after a turnaround"],"a":3,"why":"Burner light-off is infrequent, complex and has critical steps with severe consequences, all of which point to continuous use."},{"q":"Why do self-checks sometimes fail even when the person does stop and look?","opts":["People see what they expect to see, especially when components look alike and they are tired or rushed","Self-checks only work for new staff","Labels are always correct so checking them adds nothing","Self-checking only works when a second person is present"],"a":0,"why":"Expectation bias means a person can read the tag they expect rather than the one written. That is why self-checking is backed by good labelling, flagging and, for high-consequence steps, verification by a second person."}],"nfac":6},{"id":"L2M3","n":3,"t":"Verification practices: peer check, concurrent and independent verification","mins":90,"obj":"Select and perform the right verification practice for a task, and recognise and prevent rubber-stamp verification.","tools":["Verification type selector","Independent verification checklist","Rubber-stamp warning signs card"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":900,"exTitle":"The spade list signed from the control room","exTime":"40 minutes (20 group work, 10 verification drill, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L2M4","n":4,"t":"Safety-critical communication and handover","mins":110,"obj":"Use three-way communication, the phonetic alphabet and read-back for safety-critical instructions, and deliver a structured shift and permit handover combining written, verbal and walk-down elements.","tools":["Three-way communication card with phonetic alphabet","Structured shift handover template","Permit and isolation handover checklist","Override and inhibit register prompt"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"Handover at the sulphur recovery unit","exTime":"50 minutes (15 radio exercise, 25 handover role-play, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L2M5","n":5,"t":"Questioning attitude, stop when unsure, drift and flagging","mins":85,"obj":"Apply a questioning attitude and call a time-out when unsure, recognise drift and normalisation of deviance in their own work, and use flagging to prevent wrong-unit, wrong-train and wrong-component errors.","tools":["Questioning attitude trigger card","Time-out procedure card","Job-site flagging standard","Drift spotting checklist"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":900,"exTitle":"Lean amine pump B on the wrong train","exTime":"35 minutes (20 group work, 15 debrief)"},{"id":"L2M6","n":6,"t":"Situation awareness at the workface","mins":95,"obj":"Use Endsley's three levels to check situation awareness, recognise SA red flags, and apply practical techniques to manage interruptions, distraction and expectation bias in field and control room work.","tools":["Three-question SA card (What is happening? What does it mean? What next?)","SA red-flag card","Interruption management card (defer, delegate, decline)"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"Wireline and production on the same deck","exTime":"40 minutes (20 group work, 10 SA freeze-frame, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L2M7","n":7,"t":"Supervisor field skills: coaching, task observation and learning conversations","mins":95,"obj":"Coach crews in the use of HP tools, carry out a task observation that identifies error-likely situations, and lead a blame-free field learning conversation that produces action.","tools":["TWIN error precursor card","HP task observation checklist","Five-step field coaching guide","Learning conversation question card"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"Night shift at the turnaround exchanger bay","exTime":"45 minutes (15 observation, 20 coaching role-play, 10 debrief)"}],"assessment":{"summary":"Three parts. (1) A 30-question knowledge check drawn from the module quizzes and new items, closed book, 30 minutes. (2) A practical assessment of four rotating stations on Day 2 (five for supervisors), observed by a qualified assessor using the rubric below. (3) A workplace evidence logbook completed within 30 days of the course and signed off by the participant's supervisor and the site HP lead.","pass":"At least 80% (24 of 30) on the knowledge check; Competent or Strong on every practical station, with no critical fail; and a completed workplace logbook with the required entries. Supervisors must also be Competent on Station E. A participant who is Not Yet Competent on one station may be reassessed on that station once, on the day or within 30 days.","practical":null},"capstone":null});
HP.level({"id":"L3","n":3,"name":"Advanced","title":"Human Factors Analysis and Design","badge":"HP-3","tagline":"Engineers, HSE advisers and procedure writers learn to find the tasks that matter most, take them apart step by step, predict how they will fail, and design the plant, alarms and procedures so that failure is unlikely and recoverable.","audience":"Process and process safety engineers, HSE and HF advisers, operations and maintenance engineers, procedure writers and technical authors, maintenance planners, project HFE leads and discipline engineers involved in design reviews, HAZOP/LOPA facilitators and scribes, and senior operators nominated as site HP analysts.","prereq":"HP-2 (Human Performance for Supervisors) or equivalent, such as a recognised human factors foundation course plus two years' experience in operations, maintenance, engineering or process safety on a hazardous facility. Participants should be able to read a P&amp;ID and a bow-tie and have attended at least one HAZOP or LOPA. Pre-course: read the EI SCTA guidance summary and bring one task from their own site that they believe is safety critical.","duration":"3 days, 07:30-16:30 (approx. 23 h contact) plus a post-course capstone report submitted within 6 weeks (approx. 16-24 h of workplace effort).","format":"Classroom workshop, 8-12 participants in table groups of 3-4, one lead facilitator (chartered or HP-5 qualified human factors specialist) plus one co-facilitator for groups over 8. Mix of short technical inputs, worked examples, analytical group exercises on realistic plant documents (P&amp;ID extracts, procedures, alarm data, bow-ties), a field or mock-up walk-through, and a supervised capstone analysis. Spreadsheet templates for the SCT register, HTA/error analysis table and HEART calculation are issued on day 1.","outcomes":["Analyse a facility's major accident hazard bow-ties and task inventory to identify and justify a prioritised register of safety-critical tasks using explicit screening criteria.","Construct a hierarchical task analysis (HTA) with goals, sub-goals, operations and plans to an appropriate stopping level for a field or control room task.","Analyse each critical step of an HTA for credible human failures using a structured taxonomy, and evaluate consequence, recovery potential and performance influencing factors (PIFs).","Evaluate and select human factors controls using the hierarchy (eliminate, engineer, facilitate, administer) and justify them against ALARP.","Calculate a human error probability using HEART, test its sensitivity, and evaluate whether a human action may be credited as an initiating event frequency or an independent protection layer in LOPA.","Evaluate a design, control room or change against human factors engineering requirements (IOGP 454, ISO 11064, EEMUA 191 alarm performance, accessibility and labelling) and apply HF screening within management of change.","Critique and rewrite an operating procedure to a defined writing standard and plan a walk-through validation and usability test with end users.","Produce a complete safety-critical task analysis report with defensible, prioritised recommendations that site management can act on."],"competencies":[{"code":"HP3.1","statement":"Identifies and prioritises safety-critical tasks from MAH bow-ties, task inventories and HAZOP/LOPA records using documented screening criteria.","evidence":"M1 screening exercise output (SCT register extract) and capstone section 1, marked against the rubric."},{"code":"HP3.2","statement":"Produces a correct and usable HTA to an appropriate level of detail, including plans.","evidence":"M2 group HTA (filter separator) reviewed by facilitator; capstone HTA marked against the rubric."},{"code":"HP3.3","statement":"Identifies credible human failures, consequences, recovery opportunities and PIFs for each critical step.","evidence":"M3 error analysis table; capstone error analysis section."},{"code":"HP3.4","statement":"Selects and justifies proportionate HF controls using the hierarchy of control and ALARP reasoning.","evidence":"M3 and M5 exercise outputs; capstone recommendations section."},{"code":"HP3.5","statement":"Performs and critically reviews a HEART assessment and judges whether human actions can be credited in LOPA.","evidence":"M4 individual HEART calculation (to within the tolerance stated in the facilitator key) and LOPA critique; knowledge check items."},{"code":"HP3.6","statement":"Applies HFE requirements and HF screening questions to design reviews, control room and alarm changes and MoC.","evidence":"M5 MoC exercise output; knowledge check items."},{"code":"HP3.7","statement":"Writes, reviews and validates procedures to a defined standard with end users.","evidence":"M6 procedure rewrite and validation plan; capstone procedure recommendations."}],"schedule":[{"block":"Day 1 07:30-08:00","items":"Welcome, safety brief, objectives, how HP-3 builds on HP-2; participants introduce the safety-critical task they brought from site."},{"block":"Day 1 08:00-10:00","items":"M1 Identifying and screening safety-critical tasks: seven-step approach, SCT categories, task inventory, screening and prioritisation, SCT register. Exercise: Screening the Al Rimal inlet facilities."},{"block":"Day 1 10:00-10:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 1 10:15-12:15","items":"M2 Hierarchical task analysis (part 1): data collection, hierarchy, plans, stopping rule, pig launcher worked example."},{"block":"Day 1 12:15-13:00","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 1 13:00-14:00","items":"M2 (part 2) Exercise: HTA of filter separator preparation, with operator talk-through role-play, gallery critique."},{"block":"Day 1 14:00-14:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 1 14:15-16:15","items":"M3 Human error analysis, PIFs and the hierarchy of controls (part 1): SHERPA taxonomy, credibility, error analysis table, PIFs, recovery, hierarchy and ALARP; start of group work on the exercise: Draining sour water from a separator boot."},{"block":"Day 1 16:15-16:30","items":"Day review; capstone brief issued for reading."},{"block":"Day 2 07:30-08:30","items":"M3 (part 2): boot-draining exercise plenary; applying the method to two critical steps of the group's own filter separator HTA; close."},{"block":"Day 2 08:30-10:00","items":"M4 Human reliability assessment (part 1): why and when to quantify, HEART method, worked example, individual HEART calculation (P4)."},{"block":"Day 2 10:00-10:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 2 10:15-11:30","items":"M4 (part 2): limits and dependence, humans in LOPA. Exercise: Can the operator be an IPL? When not to quantify."},{"block":"Day 2 11:30-12:15","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 2 12:15-15:15","items":"M5 Human factors engineering in design and MoC: IOGP 454, access and labelling, ISO 11064, EEMUA 191 alarms, MoC HF screening. Exercise: One control room, two plants. (Break 13:45-14:00.)"},{"block":"Day 2 15:15-16:30","items":"M6 Procedure design (part 1): why procedures fail, level of use, writing standard, warnings and critical steps, verification and validation."},{"block":"Day 3 07:30-08:45","items":"M6 (part 2) Exercise: Rewrite and validate the sour water pump procedure; live walk-through with a volunteer user."},{"block":"Day 3 08:45-09:00","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 3 09:00-11:45","items":"Capstone workshop: supervised start on the PSV changeover (or approved own-site task): screening, HTA and first critical steps, with facilitator coaching at each table."},{"block":"Day 3 11:45-12:30","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 3 12:30-14:00","items":"Capstone draft findings: 10-minute presentations per participant or pair, peer and facilitator challenge."},{"block":"Day 3 14:00-14:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 3 14:15-15:00","items":"Knowledge check (30 MCQs, 40 minutes)."},{"block":"Day 3 15:00-16:00","items":"Results and feedback; capstone submission requirements and 6-week plan; how to build and maintain the site SCT register; CPD and progression to HP-4/HP-5."},{"block":"Day 3 16:00-16:30","items":"Course evaluation and close."},{"block":"Weeks 1-6 after course","items":"Capstone completed at the workplace with one optional 30-minute coaching call; report submitted and marked against the rubric; feedback within 3 weeks."}],"modules":[{"id":"L3M1","n":1,"t":"Identifying and screening safety-critical tasks","mins":120,"obj":"Build a defensible, prioritised register of safety-critical tasks by linking a site task inventory to its major accident hazards, bow-tie barriers and HAZOP/LOPA records, using explicit screening criteria.","tools":["SCT screening and prioritisation worksheet","SCT register template (spreadsheet)","Bow-tie to task mapping guide"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1200,"exTitle":"Screening the Al Rimal inlet facilities","exTime":"50 minutes"},{"id":"L3M2","n":2,"t":"Hierarchical task analysis","mins":180,"obj":"Construct a hierarchical task analysis with goals, sub-goals, operations and plans, collected from documents, observation and operators, and stopped at a level that supports error analysis.","tools":["HTA tabular template (number, step, plan, who, notes, work-as-done variation)","Walk-through and talk-through interview guide","HTA quality checklist"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1300,"exTitle":"HTA: preparing a filter separator for element change-out","exTime":"75 minutes"},{"id":"L3M3","n":3,"t":"Human error analysis, PIFs and the hierarchy of controls","mins":180,"obj":"Identify credible human failures at each critical step using a structured taxonomy, evaluate consequences, recovery and performance influencing factors, and select controls using the hierarchy of HF controls to reduce risk as low as reasonably practicable.","tools":["Human error analysis worksheet (step, failure mode, mechanism, consequence, recovery, PIFs, existing controls, recommendations)","SHERPA error mode prompt card","PIF rating checklist (job, person, organisation)","Hierarchy of HF controls card with ALARP prompts"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"Draining sour water from a separator boot","exTime":"70 minutes"},{"id":"L3M4","n":4,"t":"Human reliability assessment: HEART and LOPA","mins":165,"obj":"Calculate and critically evaluate a human error probability using HEART, and judge when and how human actions may be credited as initiating events or independent protection layers in LOPA, including when not to quantify at all.","tools":["HEART calculation spreadsheet with full GTT and EPC tables","Human IPL validation checklist (independence, time, clarity, procedure, drill, audit)","HRA sensitivity record sheet"],"learn":"\n<h2>Why quantify, and why be careful</h2>\n<p>Human reliability assessment (HRA) estimates the probability that a person will fail a task. Process safety teams ask for numbers because LOPA, QRA and SIL determination need them: an initiating event frequency for \"operator leaves drain open\", or a probability of failure on demand (PFD) for \"operator responds to high-level alarm\". Kirwan's <i>Guide to Practical Human Reliability Assessment</i> and the HSE both stress that HRA is only as good as the qualitative analysis behind it. A number without an HTA, credible failure analysis and PIF assessment is a guess with decimal places.</p>\n<div class=\"callout or\"><h4>Qualitative first, always</h4><p>Quantify only after M2 and M3 are complete. The value of HRA lies mostly in the qualitative insight and in comparing options, not in the absolute number.</p></div>\n\n<h2>HEART in outline</h2>\n<p>The Human Error Assessment and Reduction Technique (HEART) was published by Jeremy Williams in the 1980s and is widely used in the process industries because it is quick, transparent and points directly at error-reduction measures. The steps are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Classify the task into one of the <b>generic task types</b> (GTTs), which gives a nominal human error probability.</li>\n<li>Identify the relevant <b>error producing conditions</b> (EPCs), each with a maximum multiplier.</li>\n<li>For each EPC, judge the <b>assessed proportion of affect</b> (APOA), from 0 to 1.</li>\n<li>Calculate each assessed effect: <b>((maximum multiplier - 1) x APOA) + 1</b>.</li>\n<li>Multiply the nominal HEP by all the assessed effects. Cap the result at 1.0.</li></ol>\n<h3>Generic task types (nominal HEP and 5th-95th percentile bounds)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>GTT</th><th>Description (abridged)</th><th>Nominal HEP</th><th>Bounds</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>A</td><td>Totally unfamiliar, performed at speed with no real idea of likely consequences</td><td>0.55</td><td>0.35-0.97</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B</td><td>Shift or restore system to a new or original state on a single attempt without supervision or procedures</td><td>0.26</td><td>0.14-0.42</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C</td><td>Complex task requiring high level of comprehension and skill</td><td>0.16</td><td>0.12-0.28</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D</td><td>Fairly simple task performed rapidly or given scant attention</td><td>0.09</td><td>0.06-0.13</td></tr>\n<tr><td>E</td><td>Routine, highly practised, rapid task involving relatively low level of skill</td><td>0.02</td><td>0.007-0.045</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F</td><td>Restore or shift a system to original or new state following procedures, with some checking</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.0008-0.007</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G</td><td>Completely familiar, well-designed, highly practised routine task, performed several times per hour to the highest standards by highly motivated, trained and experienced staff, with time to correct errors but without significant job aids</td><td>0.0004</td><td>0.00008-0.009</td></tr>\n<tr><td>H</td><td>Respond correctly to system command even when there is an augmented or automated supervisory system providing accurate interpretation of system state</td><td>0.00002</td><td>0.000006-0.0009</td></tr>\n<tr><td>M</td><td>Miscellaneous task for which no description can be found</td><td>0.03</td><td>0.008-0.11</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Selected error producing conditions (maximum multiplier)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>EPC</th><th>Condition (abridged)</th><th>Max</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Unfamiliarity with a situation which is potentially important but which only occurs infrequently or is novel</td><td>x17</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Shortage of time available for error detection and correction</td><td>x11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Low signal-to-noise ratio</td><td>x10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Means of suppressing or overriding information or features which is too easily accessible</td><td>x9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>No means of conveying spatial and functional information in a form operators can readily assimilate</td><td>x8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Mismatch between the operator's model of the world and that imagined by the designer</td><td>x8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>No obvious means of reversing an unintended action</td><td>x8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>Channel capacity overload, particularly from simultaneous presentation of non-redundant information</td><td>x6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11</td><td>Ambiguity in the required performance standards</td><td>x5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>Mismatch between perceived and real risk</td><td>x4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>Poor, ambiguous or ill-matched system feedback</td><td>x4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>Operator inexperience (e.g. newly qualified but not expert)</td><td>x3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>Impoverished quality of information conveyed by procedures and person-to-person interaction</td><td>x3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>17</td><td>Little or no independent checking or testing of output</td><td>x3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>26</td><td>No obvious way to keep track of progress during an activity</td><td>x1.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>35</td><td>Disruption of normal work-sleep cycles</td><td>x1.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The full list has 38 EPCs; use the complete course-pack tables.</p>\n\n<h2>Worked example: opening the launcher closure under pressure</h2>\n<p>From M2 and M3: step 2.4 / 3.1, failure to confirm the barrel is depressurised before opening the closure.</p>\n<p><b>GTT F</b> (restore or shift a system following procedures, with some checking): nominal HEP 0.003.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>EPC</th><th>Max</th><th>APOA</th><th>Justification</th><th>Assessed effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>13 Poor feedback</td><td>4</td><td>0.4</td><td>Single gauge PG-106; tapping can block with debris and read zero</td><td>(3 x 0.4) + 1 = 2.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15 Inexperience</td><td>3</td><td>0.3</td><td>Contractor crew rotate; some have not used this closure type</td><td>(2 x 0.3) + 1 = 1.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12 Perceived vs real risk</td><td>4</td><td>0.2</td><td>Monthly task; 'the gauge always reads zero by now'</td><td>(3 x 0.2) + 1 = 1.6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>17 No independent check</td><td>3</td><td>0.5</td><td>No second person verifies zero pressure</td><td>(2 x 0.5) + 1 = 2.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>HEP = 0.003 x 2.2 x 1.6 x 1.6 x 2.0 = 0.003 x 11.26 = <b>0.034</b> (about 1 in 30 per operation).</p>\n<p><b>Option testing.</b> Add a second pressure indicator on the closure and a signed independent zero-pressure check: EPC 13 APOA falls to 0.1 (effect 1.3) and EPC 17 APOA to 0.1 (effect 1.2). HEP = 0.003 x 1.3 x 1.6 x 1.6 x 1.2 = 0.003 x 3.99 = <b>0.012</b>. A worthwhile improvement, roughly a factor of three. A closure interlock that cannot be released under pressure changes the question entirely: now both the interlock and the person must fail, and HEART is no longer the right tool for the combined probability.</p>\n<p><b>Sensitivity.</b> Had the team chosen GTT E (0.02) instead of F, the base-case HEP would be 0.02 x 11.26 = 0.23, nearly seven times higher. Always record why a GTT was chosen and test the alternative.</p>\n\n<h2>Limits of HRA</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Judgement-heavy:</b> GTT and APOA choices drive the answer; analysts can differ by an order of magnitude.</li>\n<li><b>Dependence:</b> successive errors by the same person or crew are not independent. An \"independent\" check by the same crew under the same time pressure is not independent. THERP's dependence model shows how much this matters: at high dependence the conditional failure probability of the second action is (1 + P)/2, i.e. at least 0.5.</li>\n<li><b>Diagnosis and violations:</b> HEART handles execution tasks better than diagnosis in complex upsets, and is weak for deliberate deviations and organisational drift.</li>\n<li><b>False precision:</b> report one significant figure and the bounds; never compare 0.034 with 0.031 as if they were different.</li></ul>\n\n<h2>Humans in LOPA</h2>\n<p><b>As initiating events.</b> An initiating event frequency = probability of failure per opportunity x number of opportunities per year. If draining is done 6 times a day (about 2,190 times a year) and the HEP of leaving the drain open too long is 0.001, the frequency is about 2 per year: a high-frequency initiating event. Published LOPA guidance (CCPS) gives generic ranges for operator failure to execute a routine procedure of around 1 in 10 to 1 in 1,000 per opportunity, depending on conditions; site-specific analysis should justify where in that range a task sits.</p>\n<p><b>As independent protection layers (IPLs).</b> Human response to an alarm is commonly credited with a PFD of 0.1, and generally no better, only when all of these hold:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The alarm is <b>independent</b> of the initiating event and of other credited IPLs (separate sensor and logic from the BPCS loop that failed).</li>\n<li>The alarm is clear, prioritised, and not lost in standing or nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>There is enough time to detect, diagnose and act: commonly at least 10 minutes from alarm to the point of no return, and more for complex diagnosis or field actions.</li>\n<li>The required action is simple, documented, trained and drilled, and the operator has the authority and the means to take it.</li>\n<li>Staffing ensures someone is available to respond at all times.</li>\n<li>Performance is auditable: alarm response is tested or drilled and records are kept.</li></ul>\n\n<h2>When not to quantify</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>When the qualitative analysis already shows reasonably practicable improvements: implement them; a number adds nothing.</li>\n<li>When the number would be used to justify not doing something that is reasonably practicable.</li>\n<li>When the dominant failure is a violation, a knowledge-based diagnosis in a novel upset, or organisational, where first-generation methods are weak.</li>\n<li>When the task is not yet understood well enough to analyse.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"src\">Based on Williams, HEART: a proposed method for assessing and reducing human error (1986) and A data-based method for assessing and reducing human error (1988); Kirwan, A Guide to Practical Human Reliability Assessment (1994); HSE RR679 Review of human reliability assessment methods (2009); Swain and Guttmann, THERP handbook NUREG/CR-1278 (1983); CCPS, Layer of Protection Analysis (2001) and Guidelines for Initiating Events and Independent Protection Layers in LOPA (2015)</p>","activity":{"title":"Can the operator be an IPL? The flare knock-out drum","time":"60 minutes","scn":"<p><span class=\"tag\">Gas plant flare system · Gulf region</span><span class=\"tag\">LOPA workshop · plant upset case</span></p>\n<p>A LOPA team is assessing the scenario \"liquid carry-over from HP flare knock-out drum D-6001 to the flare tip, causing burning liquid rain onto the plant and the adjacent road\". Initiating event: failure of the KO drum pump-out level control loop LIC-6002 (BPCS), frequency 0.1 per year. Proposed IPLs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Operator response to high-level alarm LAH-6002, PFD 0.1.</li>\n<li>Operator response to high-high-level alarm LAHH-6003 from a separate transmitter, PFD 0.1.</li>\n<li>Second pump-out pump on auto-start from LT-6003, PFD 0.1.</li></ol>\n<p>Facts gathered by the HF member of the team:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>LAH-6002 is generated from the same transmitter LT-6002 that feeds the failed control loop.</li>\n<li>From LAHH-6003 to liquid reaching the drum outlet at maximum relief load takes about 8 minutes. At normal flaring rates it takes about 40 minutes.</li>\n<li>Maximum relief loads occur during plant-wide upsets (e.g. total power failure), when the control room historically receives more than 150 alarms in the first 10 minutes.</li>\n<li>The response is to start the standby pump locally (field operator, about 6 minutes' walk) or to reduce flaring by isolating sources, which requires diagnosis.</li>\n<li>Response to LAHH-6003 is not in any procedure and has never been drilled.</li></ul>","qs":[{"q":"Which of the proposed human IPLs can be credited, and on what grounds? Test each against independence, time, clarity, procedure/training and auditability.","hint":"Shared transmitter; time at maximum load; alarm flood; field action time."},{"q":"Use HEART to estimate the HEP for the operator successfully responding to LAHH-6003 during a plant-wide upset. State your GTT and EPCs with APOAs.","hint":"Consider D or C; EPC 2 (time), EPC 8 (overload), EPC 1 (rare situation), EPC 16 (no procedure)."},{"q":"Repeat the estimate for the normal-flaring case (40 minutes available), after the site writes and drills a response procedure. What does the difference show?","hint":"Which EPCs fall away? Is a PFD of 0.1 defensible in this case?"},{"q":"What would you recommend to the LOPA team, and what should not be done with these numbers?","hint":"Split scenarios by load case; engineered alternatives; alarm rationalisation; do not stack human IPLs."}]},"quiz":[{"q":"In HEART, an EPC has a maximum multiplier of 11 and the team assesses the proportion of affect as 0.4. What is the assessed effect?","opts":["4.4","5.0","11.4","0.4"],"a":1,"why":"Assessed effect = ((11 - 1) x 0.4) + 1 = 5.0. Multiplying 11 by 0.4 directly (4.4) is a common mistake."},{"q":"What is the nominal HEP for HEART generic task type F (restore or shift a system to an original or new state following procedures, with some checking)?","opts":["0.09","0.02","0.0004","0.003"],"a":3,"why":"GTT F has a nominal HEP of 0.003 (bounds 0.0008-0.007). D is 0.09, E is 0.02 and G is 0.0004."},{"q":"A LOPA team proposes to credit operator response to a high-level alarm generated from the same transmitter as the failed level control loop. Why is this not acceptable?","opts":["Because the alarm is not independent of the initiating event.","Because operator responses can never be credited in LOPA.","Because the PFD must be at least 0.01.","Because alarms must be audible in the field."],"a":0,"why":"An IPL must be independent of the initiating event. If the transmitter fails, both the control and the alarm are lost."},{"q":"Which condition is commonly required before crediting a human response as an IPL with PFD 0.1?","opts":["The operator has more than ten years' experience.","The task is covered by a permit to work.","Sufficient time from alarm to consequence for detection, diagnosis and action, commonly at least 10 minutes, with a trained, drilled, documented response.","The site has had no incidents involving that alarm."],"a":2,"why":"Time, independence, clarity, a documented and drilled response, staffing and auditability are the usual conditions; experience and incident history are not substitutes."},{"q":"A routine draining task is performed 2,000 times a year with an estimated HEP of 0.001 for leaving the drain open too long. What is the initiating event frequency?","opts":["0.001 per year","2 per year","0.5 per year","2,000 per year"],"a":1,"why":"Frequency = HEP per opportunity x opportunities per year = 0.001 x 2,000 = 2 per year."},{"q":"When is quantification of human error least useful?","opts":["When comparing two design options for the same task.","When a LOPA requires a justified initiating event frequency.","When checking whether a human response IPL is credible.","When the qualitative analysis has already identified reasonably practicable improvements that should simply be implemented."],"a":3,"why":"If reasonably practicable improvements are known, implement them. Quantifying risks delaying action or being used to argue against it."}],"nfac":6},{"id":"L3M5","n":5,"t":"Human factors engineering in design and management of change","mins":165,"obj":"Evaluate projects, control rooms, alarm systems and plant changes against human factors engineering requirements, and apply HF screening within management of change so that new error traps are not built in.","tools":["MoC HF screening checklist (10 questions)","Alarm performance KPI sheet (EEMUA 191 benchmarks)","3D model review HFE checklist (access, valves, labelling, maintenance)"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1300,"exTitle":"One control room, two plants","exTime":"60 minutes"},{"id":"L3M6","n":6,"t":"Procedure design, verification and validation","mins":150,"obj":"Critique and rewrite an operating procedure to a defined writing standard, mark critical steps, and plan and run a talk-through or walk-through validation and usability test with the people who will use it.","tools":["Procedure writing standard (one-page)","Procedure review checklist (verification)","Walk-through and usability test observation sheet (validation)","Critical step and hold point marking convention"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"Rewrite and validate: returning a sour water pump to service","exTime":"65 minutes"}],"assessment":{"summary":"Three parts. (1) A closed-book knowledge check of 30 multiple-choice questions drawn from the six module banks (minimum four per module), taken on day 3. (2) In-course practical outputs assessed by the facilitator: the M1 SCT screening, the M2 HTA, the M3 error analysis, an individual HEART calculation in M4, and the M6 procedure rewrite. (3) The capstone: a full safety-critical task analysis on a supplied or own-site task, started in a supervised workshop on day 3 and submitted as a written report within 6 weeks, marked against the published rubric by an HP-5 or chartered human factors assessor.","pass":"80% or more on the knowledge check (24 of 30) AND 'Competent' on every in-course practical criterion AND 'Competent' or better on every capstone rubric criterion. 'Distinction' is awarded where at least five capstone criteria are marked Distinction and none below Competent. One re-sit of the knowledge check is allowed; a capstone marked Not Yet Competent on any criterion may be resubmitted once within 4 weeks of feedback.","practical":null},"capstone":{"title":"Safety-critical task analysis: PSV changeover on a sour inlet separator","deliverables":["SCTA report (12 to 20 pages plus appendices) with a one-page executive summary for the site manager.","HTA in tabular form (and optionally a diagram) with plans, roles and work-as-done variations.","Human error analysis table covering every critical step: failure mode code, mechanism, consequence, recovery, PIF ratings, existing controls and recommendations.","Quantification decision record: HEART worksheet with sensitivity tests, or a written justification for not quantifying.","Recommendations register with hierarchy level, ALARP justification, owner role, priority and completion criterion.","Revised procedure extract for the critical steps to the M6 writing standard, plus a validation plan.","10-minute presentation of findings to the course group on day 3 (draft findings) and, where possible, to the site's responsible manager."],"brief":null}});
HP.level({"id":"L4","n":4,"name":"Leader","title":"Leading Human Performance","badge":"HP-4","tagline":"Leaders do not manage behaviour directly; they manage the conditions that produce it. This level equips the people who set budgets, schedules, staffing and consequences to shape reliable performance, respond fairly to failure, learn from normal work and see drift before it becomes a disaster.","audience":"Superintendents, area and operations managers, maintenance managers, plant managers, HSE managers and members of the asset leadership team, including contractor site managers who hold equivalent authority over resources, schedules and consequences.","prereq":"HP-1 Human Performance Foundations (or equivalent). HP-2 Human Performance Tools in Practice is strongly recommended. Participants must hold, or be about to take up, a role with authority over resources, schedules, staffing or consequence management, and must bring a short profile of their area (recent events, top risks, staffing pattern, key indicators) to Day 1.","duration":"2 days (approximately 16 h contact including assessment and the capstone panel), plus a 90-day follow-up review","format":"Face-to-face, 8 to 16 participants, ideally a mix of operations, maintenance, technical and HSE leaders from the same asset so that real decisions can be discussed. Highly discussion-based: case analysis, decision simulations, a field engagement practice walk (live plant or a mock-up if the venue is off-site), consequence-management panels and a capstone 90-day plan presented to a panel that includes a senior sponsor. Co-delivered by an HP-5 qualified facilitator and a senior line leader. Chatham House Rule applies to all discussion of real site events.","outcomes":["Analyse how their own decisions on budgets, schedules, staffing, deferrals, contracts and targets shape the performance shaping factors experienced at the sharp end, and identify where production pressure is eroding protection in their area.","Apply a just culture decision process (Reason culpability tree, Marx behavioural categories and the substitution test) consistently to real cases, distinguishing human error, at-risk behaviour and reckless behaviour independently of outcome, rank or employer.","Design and lead a response to failure, including the first 48 hours after a significant event, that cares for people, protects learning and avoids premature blame, and compare retributive and restorative responses.","Plan and conduct a field engagement visit and sponsor a learning team that uncovers work-as-done, and convert what is learned into system improvements with visible feedback to the workforce.","Evaluate weak signals of organisational drift and normalisation of deviance in their area using the Rasmussen drift model and HRO principles, drawing on the lessons of Texas City, Longford and Deepwater Horizon.","Critique an existing safety and human performance scorecard for perverse incentives and under-reporting effects, and construct a balanced set of leading and lagging human performance indicators linked to critical risk controls.","Create a leader standard work routine and make defensible decisions on staffing, fatigue, turnaround scheduling and start-up readiness under production pressure, documenting the risk reasoning.","Create and present a 90-day leadership human performance action plan for their own area, with measurable commitments, owners and a method for checking that it worked."],"competencies":[{"code":"HP4.1","statement":"Recognises and manages the influence of leadership decisions on performance shaping factors and the balance of production and protection.","evidence":"Module 1 case analysis; PSF-to-decision map for own area included in the capstone plan."},{"code":"HP4.2","statement":"Applies just and fair culture principles consistently, separating the assessment of behaviour from the severity of the outcome, and uses restorative as well as accountability-based responses.","evidence":"Consequence-management panel in the practical assessment scored against the rubric; knowledge check items."},{"code":"HP4.3","statement":"Responds to failure in a way that protects people, reporting and learning, including clear and blame-free communication after an event.","evidence":"Event-response simulation in the practical assessment: first-call handling, first-48-hours plan and workforce message."},{"code":"HP4.4","statement":"Learns from normal work through well-conducted field engagement and sponsorship of learning teams, and closes the loop with the workforce.","evidence":"Observed practice field engagement with learning conversation notes; at least six workplace field engagement visits (two or more on nights or weekends) and one sponsored learning team logged within 90 days."},{"code":"HP4.5","statement":"Detects and acts on weak signals of drift and normalisation of deviance using HRO principles.","evidence":"Module 4 drift analysis; weak-signal review routine included in leader standard work; capstone section on drift."},{"code":"HP4.6","statement":"Selects and uses human performance and process safety indicators that drive learning rather than under-reporting, and judges cultural maturity from evidence.","evidence":"Module 5 dashboard redesign; indicator set in the capstone plan reviewed by the panel."},{"code":"HP4.7","statement":"Sponsors a human performance programme and practises leader standard work, including defensible decisions on staffing, fatigue, turnarounds and critical-task ownership.","evidence":"Capstone 90-day plan presented to and accepted by the panel; 90-day follow-up review of delivery."}],"schedule":[{"block":"Day 1 07:30-08:00","items":"Opening by the senior sponsor; course objectives; participants share their area profile in one minute each; capstone briefing."},{"block":"Day 1 08:00-09:50","items":"M1 How leaders create the conditions: PSFs and leadership decisions, production and protection, ETTO, local rationality, the response-to-failure moment. Exercise: the pig receiver that would not bleed."},{"block":"Day 1 09:50-10:05","items":"Break"},{"block":"Day 1 10:05-12:05","items":"M2 Just and fair culture: Marx categories, Reason culpability decision tree, substitution test, restorative and retributive responses, consistency. Exercise: consequence-management panels."},{"block":"Day 1 12:05-12:50","items":"Lunch and prayer"},{"block":"Day 1 12:50-14:40","items":"M3 Learning from normal work: Safety-II, varieties of work, field engagement, learning teams. Exercise: propane bullet draining, engagement role-play or live field visit."},{"block":"Day 1 14:40-14:55","items":"Break"},{"block":"Day 1 14:55-15:30","items":"Practical Part A: individual decision record on an unseen just culture case."},{"block":"Day 1 15:30-16:15","items":"Capstone workshop 1: PSF-to-decision map for own area and first draft of priorities, with peer challenge in pairs."},{"block":"Day 1 16:15-16:30","items":"Day 1 review: one thing each participant will do differently at their next morning meeting."},{"block":"Day 2 07:30-07:40","items":"Recap and overnight reflections."},{"block":"Day 2 07:40-09:30","items":"M4 Organisational drift and weak signals: normalisation of deviance, Rasmussen, chronic unease, HRO principles, Texas City, Longford, Deepwater Horizon. Exercise: two years on a mature offshore platform."},{"block":"Day 2 09:30-09:45","items":"Break"},{"block":"Day 2 09:45-11:20","items":"M5 Measuring human performance: dual assurance, API RP 754 tiers, HP indicators, perverse incentives, Hudson and Westrum. Exercise: the regional scorecard proposal."},{"block":"Day 2 11:20-12:05","items":"Lunch and prayer"},{"block":"Day 2 12:05-13:55","items":"M6 Leader standard work and programme sponsorship: decisions under pressure, staffing and fatigue, turnarounds and start-up, critical task ownership, communicating after an event. Exercise: day 24 of a hydrocracker turnaround."},{"block":"Day 2 13:55-14:35","items":"Practical Part B: individual event-response simulation with timed injects."},{"block":"Day 2 14:35-15:00","items":"Knowledge check: 25 questions, closed book."},{"block":"Day 2 15:00-17:00","items":"Capstone panels: 90-day plan presentations (8 minutes plus 7 minutes challenge), two panels in parallel, each with a senior sponsor (course sponsor or delegate) and a facilitator."},{"block":"Day 2 17:00-17:15","items":"Sponsor commitments, 90-day review arrangements and course close."},{"block":"Day 90","items":"Follow-up review (60 to 90 minutes per participant, or as a cohort workshop): workplace evidence, progress against the plan, certificate decision."}],"modules":[{"id":"L4M1","n":1,"t":"How leaders create the conditions for performance","mins":110,"obj":"Explain how leadership decisions set the performance shaping factors at the sharp end, recognise the production-protection balance and efficiency-thoroughness trade-offs in their own area, and plan the response-to-failure moment.","tools":["PSF-to-decision map (leader version)","First-48-hours leader checklist","Production-protection signal audit (agendas, emails, visits)"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1400,"exTitle":"The pig receiver that would not bleed","exTime":"50 minutes (25 group work, 15 plenary, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L4M2","n":2,"t":"Just and fair culture","mins":120,"obj":"Apply a just culture decision process consistently to real cases, separating the judgement of behaviour from the outcome, and choose between accountability-based and restorative responses.","tools":["Culpability decision tree worksheet (Reason)","Substitution test peer-interview guide","Consistency checklist for consequence decisions","Restorative conversation guide"],"learn":"\n<h2>Why leaders need a just culture</h2>\n<p>Reason (1997) described a safety culture as an informed culture, built from four connected parts: a <b>reporting culture</b> in which people report errors and near misses, a <b>just culture</b> that makes it safe to do so, a <b>flexible culture</b> that can adapt under pressure, and a <b>learning culture</b> that draws the right conclusions and acts on them. Reporting depends on trust, and trust depends on the workforce believing that leaders will judge fairly. Every disciplinary decision a leader makes is a message to everyone who hears about it.</p>\n<p>A just culture is not a no-blame culture. Reason was clear that a blanket amnesty is neither credible nor fair: a small proportion of acts are reckless or malicious and deserve sanction. The task is to draw the line between acceptable and unacceptable behaviour in the right place, and to draw it the same way every time.</p>\n<h2>Three kinds of behaviour (Marx)</h2>\n<p>David Marx's just culture model separates the quality of the choice from the severity of the outcome. The response depends on the behaviour, not on whether anyone was hurt.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Behaviour</th><th>What it is</th><th>Oil and gas example</th><th>Appropriate response</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Human error</b></td><td>Inadvertent action: a slip, lapse or mistake. The person did not intend to do the wrong thing.</td><td>An operator opens the drain on Train B instead of Train A on mirror-image trains with faded tags.</td><td><b>Console</b> the person and fix the system: labelling, layout, verification, workload.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>At-risk behaviour</b></td><td>A choice that increases risk where the risk is not recognised or is mistakenly believed to be justified. Usually drift, often widespread.</td><td>Confirming zero pressure on a pig receiver using the gauge alone because the bleed screw has been seized for months and \"everyone does it\".</td><td><b>Coach</b>: make the risk visible, remove the incentives for the shortcut, make the right way the easy way.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reckless behaviour</b></td><td>A conscious disregard of a substantial and unjustifiable risk.</td><td>A supervisor sends a crew into a vessel knowing the gas test has not been done, telling them it will be fine.</td><td><b>Sanction</b> through a fair, consistent disciplinary process.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Marx later built these categories into a structured decision aid, often called the Just Culture Algorithm, which starts by asking which duty was breached (a duty to follow a procedural rule, a duty to avoid causing unjustifiable risk or harm, or a duty to produce an outcome) before classifying the behaviour. Whichever aid a site adopts, Reason's tree below or a Marx-based algorithm, use one consistently.</p>\n<p>The same outcome can follow any of the three. The same behaviour can produce no harm one day and a fatality the next. If leaders let the outcome decide the response, they punish bad luck and reward good luck, and the workforce learns to hide.</p>\n<h2>The culpability decision tree (Reason)</h2>\n<p>Reason's decision tree gives leaders a structured way to work through an unsafe act. The tests are taken in order and each one is asked about a specific act by a specific person.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Deliberate harm test:</b> were the actions intended, and were the consequences intended? If both, this is sabotage or malicious damage and becomes a matter for disciplinary and possibly legal action. This is very rare.</li>\n<li><b>Incapacity test:</b> was the person affected by a medical condition, illness or substance? If so, was it known or self-inflicted? Fatigue imposed by the roster is a system condition, not personal incapacity.</li>\n<li><b>Foresight test:</b> did the person knowingly violate a safe operating procedure? If so, were the procedures available, workable, intelligible and correct? If they were not, this is a <b>system-induced violation</b>. If they were, it is a <b>possible reckless violation</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Substitution test:</b> would another person with similar qualifications and experience, in the same circumstances, probably have done the same thing? If yes, the act is a <b>blameless error</b>. If no, were there deficiencies in training, selection or experience? If there were, it is system-induced; if not, a <b>possible negligent error</b>.</li>\n<li><b>History test:</b> does the person have a history of unsafe acts? This does not change the classification of this act, but may indicate the need for corrective training, counselling or closer supervision.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout or\"><h4>The substitution test in practice</h4><p>Reason credits Johnston with the substitution test. Mentally substitute another competent person from the same crew, with the same training, time pressure, information and equipment, and ask: given how events unfolded and were perceived at the time, is it probable that they would have behaved differently? Better still, ask three or four real peers. If their honest answer is \"I would probably have done the same\", the problem lies in the system and punishing the individual will not prevent recurrence.</p></div>\n<h2>Retributive and restorative responses (Dekker)</h2>\n<p>Dekker contrasts two ways of doing justice after an event. A <b>retributive</b> just culture asks which rule was broken, who did it, how bad the breach was and what the consequence should be. A <b>restorative</b> just culture asks who has been hurt, what their needs are, and whose obligation it is to meet those needs. Both have a place. A restorative approach is especially valuable where the act was an error or at-risk behaviour, where the person involved is also a victim (the \"second victim\"), and where trust between the workforce and management has been damaged.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Retributive questions</th><th>Restorative questions</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Which rule was broken?</td><td>Who is hurt by this event?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Who did it?</td><td>What are their needs?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How bad was the breach?</td><td>Whose obligation is it to meet those needs?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What should the consequence be?</td><td>How do we involve the people affected in putting things right and learning?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Consistent consequence management</h2>\n<p>Inconsistency destroys a just culture faster than any single bad decision. Leaders should test every decision against five consistency checks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Outcome:</b> would we respond the same way if nobody had been hurt, or if someone had been killed? Outcome bias and hindsight bias push towards harsher judgement of acts that happened to have bad outcomes.</li>\n<li><b>Rank:</b> would we respond the same way to a superintendent as to a field operator?</li>\n<li><b>Employer:</b> would we respond the same way to a contractor as to an employee? Contract clauses that allow \"removal from site\" can impose a far heavier consequence on a contract worker than an employee would face for the same act; in the region this can also affect a worker's employment and residence status. Company and contractor must apply the same just culture process.</li>\n<li><b>Visibility:</b> would we respond the same way if the act had been discovered by audit rather than self-reported? Self-reporting should be recognised.</li>\n<li><b>Precedent:</b> what did we do last time? Keep a confidential register of decisions and their reasoning so that panels can check consistency.</li></ul>\n<h3>Making it work</h3>\n<p>Put the decision process in a written policy that the workforce has seen. Use a small panel rather than one manager: the line manager, HR, an HP or HSE practitioner, and a respected peer of the person involved. Record the tests and the reasoning. Time-box the process, because weeks of uncertainty are themselves a punishment. Tell the person and, as far as confidentiality allows, the workforce what was decided and why. Above all, when the tree points to the system, the leader owns the system action.</p>\n<div class=\"rev\"><button type=\"button\">The outcome was severe. Does that not make it more culpable?</button><div class=\"body\"><p>No. Culpability lies in the choice and the knowledge the person had at the time, not in what happened afterwards. A severe outcome justifies a deeper investigation and more urgent system action. It does not turn an error into a violation. If leaders punish severity, people learn that the safest course is to hide the near misses that could have warned everyone.</p></div></div>\n<h3>Sort it</h3><p>Classify each behaviour using the Marx categories.</p>\n<div class=\"sortq\" data-sort='[[\"Operator selects the wrong pump on a twin set with identical tags\",\"Human error\"],[\"Technician leaves a gas detector inhibited overnight because the crew normally finish calibration next shift\",\"At-risk behaviour\"],[\"Supervisor knowingly sends a crew into a vessel with no gas test because the tester is late\",\"Reckless behaviour\"],[\"Experienced operator stops using the pigging checklist because the job feels routine\",\"At-risk behaviour\"],[\"Fitter misreads a torque value from a smudged datasheet\",\"Human error\"],[\"Driver repeatedly overrides the vehicle speed limiter on site after being warned twice\",\"Reckless behaviour\"]]' data-opts='[\"Human error\",\"At-risk behaviour\",\"Reckless behaviour\"]'></div>\n<p class=\"src\">Based on Reason (1997) Managing the Risks of Organizational Accidents (safety culture components and culpability decision tree); Marx, Patient Safety and the Just Culture; Dekker, Just Culture: Restoring Trust and Accountability in Your Organization; Energy Institute Human Factors Briefing Note on just culture; IOGP Report 621 Demystifying human factors.</p>","activity":{"title":"Three cases on the consequence-management panel","time":"55 minutes (30 panels in threes, 15 plenary, 10 debrief)","scn":"<p><span class=\"tag\">Sour gas processing plant with sulphur recovery · Gulf region</span><span class=\"tag\">Monthly consequence-management panel · plant turnaround just completed</span></p>\n<p>The plant manager has asked the panel (operations manager, maintenance manager, HSE manager and an HR adviser) to recommend responses to three cases from the last month.</p>\n<p><b>Case A: solo sour sampling.</b> At 02:30 a company field operator with nine years' service took a sample from the sour water stripper feed. The procedure requires a standby person and supplied breathing air. The second operator had been called to an ESD trip on the inlet compressor, and the lab had flagged the sample as overdue. The sample cylinder fitting leaked; his personal monitor alarmed and he withdrew without injury. He reported it himself. Asked privately, four of five operators say they have sampled alone on nights when the second operator was busy.</p>\n<p><b>Case B: isolation signed without a field walk.</b> On day 16 of a turnaround run on 14-hour shifts, a contractor mechanical supervisor signed the isolation verification for a reboiler pump from the permit office without walking the line. A drain valve was open; a fitter noticed product at the drain before breaking the flange and stopped the job. During the previous turnaround this supervisor was coached once for the same practice. His employer, the turnaround contractor, has already removed him from site under its zero-tolerance policy and the company has not intervened.</p>\n<p><b>Case C: detectors left inhibited.</b> A company instrument technician inhibited two H₂S detectors in the compressor house to stop nuisance alarms while working on a nearby analyser. He did not use the override permit and did not tell the control room. He went off shift without removing the inhibits. They were found 52 hours later in a control room audit. The override process takes about five minutes and is used routinely by other technicians. A colleague reports that he said \"nobody checks the inhibit list anyway\". Nobody was harmed.</p>","qs":[{"q":"1. Take each case through Reason's culpability decision tree, stating the answer to each test and the resulting classification.","hint":"Work through deliberate harm, incapacity, foresight (including whether the procedure was workable), substitution and history, in order."},{"q":"2. Apply the substitution test to Cases A and B. How would you collect the evidence for it in practice?","hint":"Who are the real peers, what would you ask them, and how would you protect them from feeling they are informing?"},{"q":"3. Recommend a response for each person and the system actions that leaders own. Where would a restorative approach help?","hint":"Separate individual and system actions. Use the restorative questions for at least one case."},{"q":"4. Test your recommendations for consistency. Would they change if Case C had caused a fatality, or if Case B had involved a company superintendent?","hint":"Address outcome bias, rank and employer consistency explicitly, including the contractor's removal decision."}]},"quiz":[{"q":"In Reason's model of an informed safety culture, what does a just culture enable most directly?","opts":["A flexible culture during emergencies","Lower insurance premiums","A reporting culture, because people trust that they will be treated fairly","Elimination of all disciplinary action"],"a":2,"why":"People report errors and near misses only if they trust that leaders will judge fairly. The just culture underpins the reporting culture, which feeds the learning culture."},{"q":"Under Marx's model, which response fits at-risk behaviour?","opts":["Coach: make the risk visible and remove the incentives for the shortcut","Console the individual and take no further action","Sanction through disciplinary action","Dismiss to set an example"],"a":0,"why":"At-risk behaviour is a choice whose risk is not recognised or is believed to be justified, usually drift. Coaching plus system change is the appropriate response."},{"q":"In the foresight test of Reason's decision tree, a knowing violation of a procedure that was not workable or correct is classified as:","opts":["Sabotage","A possible negligent error","A blameless error","A system-induced violation"],"a":3,"why":"If the procedure was not available, workable, intelligible and correct, the violation is system-induced. Only if it was all of those does the tree point to a possible reckless violation."},{"q":"What is the purpose of the substitution test?","opts":["To find a replacement for the person involved","To judge whether another similarly qualified and experienced person in the same circumstances would probably have acted the same way","To decide whether the procedure needs to be rewritten","To compare the cost of the event with the cost of prevention"],"a":1,"why":"The substitution test separates individual culpability from system influence. If peers would probably have done the same, punishing the individual will not prevent recurrence."},{"q":"Two identical acts of signing an isolation without a field walk occur. One leads to a release, the other to nothing. What should a just culture panel do?","opts":["Apply the same classification to both, while investigating the release in more depth","Discipline only the person whose act led to the release","Discipline both more severely because of the release","Take no action on either because the outcomes differ"],"a":0,"why":"Culpability depends on the choice and knowledge at the time, not on the outcome. Outcome bias leads to inconsistent and unfair decisions."},{"q":"Which question belongs to a restorative rather than a retributive response?","opts":["Which rule was broken?","How serious was the breach?","Who has been hurt, and what do they need?","What should the penalty be?"],"a":2,"why":"Dekker's restorative approach asks who is hurt, what their needs are and whose obligation it is to meet them. The others are retributive questions."}],"nfac":6},{"id":"L4M3","n":3,"t":"Learning from normal work","mins":110,"obj":"Conduct field engagement that uncovers work-as-done rather than inspecting compliance, and sponsor a learning team whose findings change the system and are fed back to the workforce.","tools":["Field engagement visit card (open questions and follow-up log)","Learning team sponsor guide","Learning team facilitation outline (two sessions with soak time)","You said, we did feedback template"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1200,"exTitle":"Draining water from the propane bullets","exTime":"50 minutes (25 group work, 15 practice conversation, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L4M4","n":4,"t":"Organisational drift and weak signals","mins":110,"obj":"Recognise the mechanisms of drift and normalisation of deviance, detect weak signals in their own area using HRO principles, and draw accurate leadership lessons from Texas City, Longford and Deepwater Horizon.","tools":["Weak-signal review template (barrier status trends)","Rasmussen drift map worksheet","HRO principles leader self-assessment"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1400,"exTitle":"Two years on a mature offshore platform","exTime":"50 minutes (25 group analysis, 15 plenary, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L4M5","n":5,"t":"Measuring human performance","mins":95,"obj":"Select leading and lagging indicators that show the health of human performance and critical barriers without driving under-reporting, and judge cultural maturity from evidence rather than self-assessment.","tools":["Indicator design canvas (risk control, leading, lagging, gaming risk, safeguard)","Perverse incentive check","Hudson ladder and Westrum evidence worksheet"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"The regional scorecard proposal","exTime":"45 minutes (25 group work, 10 plenary, 10 debrief)"},{"id":"L4M6","n":6,"t":"Leader standard work and HP programme sponsorship","mins":110,"obj":"Build leader routines that keep attention on protection, make and record defensible decisions on staffing, fatigue, turnarounds and start-up under production pressure, communicate well after an event, and sponsor a human performance programme that changes conditions rather than running a campaign.","tools":["Leader standard work template (daily, weekly, monthly)","Barrier questions and decision record card","Post-event message structure (People, Facts, Unknowns, Actions, Ask, Next update)","HP programme sponsor checklist"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1400,"exTitle":"Day 24 of a hydrocracker turnaround","exTime":"50 minutes (25 group work, 15 plenary, 10 debrief)"}],"assessment":{"summary":"Five parts. (1) A 25-question knowledge check drawn from the module question bank (new items, not the module quizzes verbatim), closed book, 25 minutes, on Day 2. (2) Practical Part A: an individual written decision record on an unseen just culture case (Day 1). (3) Practical Part B: an individual event-response simulation with timed injects (Day 2). (4) The capstone 90-day leadership HP action plan, presented to a panel that includes a senior sponsor. (5) A 90-day follow-up review of workplace evidence with the HP lead and the participant's own line manager. The HP-4 certificate is issued after the 90-day review.","pass":"At least 80% (20 of 25) on the knowledge check; Competent or better on every criterion of Practical Parts A and B with no critical fail; Competent or better on every capstone criterion; and a 90-day review confirming the required workplace evidence. A participant who is Not Yet Competent on Part A or Part B may resubmit once within 30 days. A capstone plan judged Not Yet Competent may be revised and re-presented to the HP lead and sponsor within 30 days.","practical":null},"capstone":{"title":"Leadership HP action plan: my first 90 days","deliverables":["A one-page PSF-to-decision map for the participant's area","The 90-day plan on the provided template (maximum four pages), including indicators with gaming safeguards","Personal leader standard work (one page)","An 8-minute presentation to the panel","Written sponsor commitments agreed at the panel, returned to the participant within one week","Evidence pack for the 90-day follow-up review"],"brief":null}});
HP.level({"id":"L5","n":5,"name":"Master","title":"Human Performance Lead (Master Practitioner)","badge":"HP-5","tagline":"The people who hold this level own the human performance programme: they lead systemic investigations, choose and apply systems analysis methods, assess and design site programmes, verify human-dependent barriers, judge organisational change, facilitate HP-1 to HP-4 and persuade executives to keep investing when the incident rate is quiet.","audience":"Designated human performance leads and champions, senior human factors specialists, lead incident investigators, process safety and HSE leads who will own the site or company HP programme, and the in-house facilitators who will deliver and assess HP-1 to HP-4.","prereq":"HP-3 Human Factors Analysis and Design AND HP-4 Leading Human Performance; or HP-3 plus at least three years in a leadership role with authority over resources or a lead investigator role (evidenced by CV and line manager statement). Participants must have participated in at least two incident investigations and must agree a workplace capstone topic and sponsor with their line manager before Day 1, bringing a two-page asset profile (major accident hazards, organisation chart, recent significant events, current HP activities).","duration":"4 days face-to-face (approximately 31 h contact), followed by a 3-month workplace capstone (approximately 60 to 80 h of workplace effort), an observed HP-1 facilitation and a 60-minute panel viva.","format":"Masterclass format for 6 to 10 participants, delivered by a chartered human factors specialist (or HP-5 holder accredited as assessor) with a second facilitator for practice sessions. Heavy use of case material: interview transcripts, DCS trends and alarm logs, permit and isolation records, organisational charts and staffing data from composite Gulf-region assets. Includes a live cognitive interview practice with a trained role-player, a facilitation micro-teach filmed for feedback, an executive briefing simulation with a role-played asset manager, and supervised capstone planning. Pre-reading (approximately 6 h) is issued three weeks before Day 1. Chatham House rule applies to all discussion of real site events.","outcomes":["Lead a systemic human factors investigation of a significant process safety event, planning and conducting cognitive interviews, reconstructing the timeline from multiple data sources and writing findings free of hindsight bias, outcome bias and counterfactual language.","Classify contributing factors using HFACS (and its oil and gas adaptation HFACS-OGI) and evaluate the strengths and limits of taxonomy-based classification against local rationality reasoning.","Select and apply an appropriate systems analysis method (AcciMap, STAMP/CAST or FRAM) for a given investigative or proactive question, and justify the choice to a sponsor.","Assess the maturity of a site human factors programme against the HSE key human factors topics and good practice from Energy Institute guidance, and design a prioritised, resourced roadmap integrated with the process safety management system (CCPS RBPS or SEMS).","Facilitate learning teams and deliver HP-1 to HP-4 to the pathway delivery standard, using adult learning principles, and coach leaders through difficult conversations about failure.","Evaluate human-dependent barriers in bow-ties and LOPA, write barrier performance standards for human actions, design verification activities and critique human reliability assessments for misuse.","Evaluate organisational change, staffing arrangements and fatigue risk using structured methods (scenario-based staffing assessment, resilience analysis, FRMS design) and recommend defensible decisions.","Construct and present a business case and change plan for sustaining the HP programme, using Kotter and ADKAR, which a senior executive can act on."],"competencies":[{"code":"HP5.1","statement":"Leads systemic human factors investigations to a recognised standard, including interviewing, timeline reconstruction and bias-free findings.","evidence":"Observed cognitive interview (M1), timeline and findings exercise, and either the capstone investigation report or the investigation section of the viva."},{"code":"HP5.2","statement":"Selects and applies systems analysis methods (AcciMap, CAST, FRAM) appropriately and explains their limits.","evidence":"M2 group analysis with individual method-selection justification; capstone method section; viva questions."},{"code":"HP5.3","statement":"Assesses human factors programme maturity and designs a governed, resourced roadmap integrated with process safety management.","evidence":"M3 gap assessment exercise; capstone gap assessment and roadmap (option A) or the improvement plan arising from the investigation (option B)."},{"code":"HP5.4","statement":"Facilitates HP-1 to HP-4 and learning teams to the pathway delivery standard and coaches leaders effectively.","evidence":"Filmed micro-teach in M4 with peer and tutor feedback; observed HP-1 delivery at the workplace scored by an accredited assessor."},{"code":"HP5.5","statement":"Specifies, verifies and critiques human-dependent barriers and human reliability claims.","evidence":"M5 barrier performance standard exercise; knowledge check; viva question on a supplied LOPA or bow-tie."},{"code":"HP5.6","statement":"Evaluates organisational change, staffing, fatigue and resilience and advises on defensible decisions.","evidence":"M6 staffing assessment exercise and written recommendation; capstone sections on organisational factors."},{"code":"HP5.7","statement":"Influences senior leaders and sustains the HP programme through a credible business case and change plan.","evidence":"M7 executive briefing simulation scored against the rubric; capstone roadmap governance and sustainment section; panel viva."}],"schedule":[{"block":"Pre-course (3 weeks before)","items":"Capstone charter agreed with sponsor; pre-reading (approx. 6 h): IOGP 621 summary, CAST handbook introduction, Bow Ties in Risk Management extract, HSE CRR 348 summary; bring an anonymised investigation report, a bow-tie or LOPA from site and the two-page asset profile."},{"block":"Day 1 07:30-08:00","items":"Welcome, safety brief, the HP-5 role, assessment and capstone overview; participants present their asset profile in two minutes each."},{"block":"Day 1 08:00-10:00","items":"M1 Systemic incident investigation (part 1): role of the HF lead, IOGP 621, five biases, planning the HF strand, local rationality, cognitive interview technique."},{"block":"Day 1 10:00-10:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 1 10:15-12:15","items":"M1 (part 2): live cognitive interview practice with a role-player (assessed P1); timeline reconstruction; HFACS and HFACS-OGI; Wadi Saham scenario."},{"block":"Day 1 12:15-13:00","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 1 13:00-13:30","items":"M1 (part 3): bias hunt on participants' own investigation reports; close."},{"block":"Day 1 13:30-15:00","items":"M2 Systems analysis methods (part 1): limits of linear models, AcciMap, STAMP and CAST with Buncefield, FRAM."},{"block":"Day 1 15:00-15:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 1 15:15-16:30","items":"M2 (part 2): Khor Taiba overfill in three lenses (group work)."},{"block":"Day 2 07:30-08:15","items":"M2 (part 3): cross-critique and plenary; method selection; method-selection justification (assessed P2)."},{"block":"Day 2 08:15-10:00","items":"M5 Advanced HRA and human barrier management (part 1): barrier tests, performance standards, verification, alarm response as a barrier, HRA critique and LOPA."},{"block":"Day 2 10:00-10:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 2 10:15-12:00","items":"M5 (part 2): Hamra gas blow-by scenario; review of own bow-ties and LOPAs; barrier performance standard (assessed P3)."},{"block":"Day 2 12:00-12:45","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 2 12:45-14:15","items":"M3 HP programme design and maturity assessment (part 1): reference framework, gap assessment method, maturity scale, roadmap, RBPS and SEMS integration, resourcing."},{"block":"Day 2 14:15-14:30","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 2 14:30-16:00","items":"M3 (part 2): Ras Bandar gap assessment and roadmap; capstone option A scoping."},{"block":"Day 2 16:00-16:30","items":"Reflection and capstone peer check-in."},{"block":"Day 3 07:30-10:00","items":"M6 Organisational factors and resilience engineering (part 1): Safety-II, ETTO, four potentials and RAG, HRO principles, organisational change and Longford, staffing assessment (CRR 348), FRMS design."},{"block":"Day 3 10:00-10:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 3 10:15-11:15","items":"M6 (part 2): Umm Lasafa scenario with plot-plan walk-through; staffing recommendation (assessed P4); own-asset RAG."},{"block":"Day 3 11:15-12:00","items":"Capstone clinic 1: option B scoping, data access and interview ethics."},{"block":"Day 3 12:00-12:45","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 3 12:45-15:00","items":"M4 Facilitation and coaching mastery (part 1): adult learning, delivery standard, managing the room, learning teams, GROW, difficult conversations; Jebel Nur role-plays in trios."},{"block":"Day 3 15:00-15:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 3 15:15-16:30","items":"M4 (part 2): filmed micro-teach round 1 (formative)."},{"block":"Day 4 07:30-08:00","items":"M4 (part 3): micro-teach round 2 feedback; planning the observed HP-1."},{"block":"Day 4 08:00-10:00","items":"M7 Influencing and change leadership (part 1): business case, executive communication, Kotter, ADKAR, sustaining the programme; preparation for the executive briefing."},{"block":"Day 4 10:00-10:15","items":"Break."},{"block":"Day 4 10:15-11:15","items":"M7 (part 2): Barr Al Qasr executive briefing simulation (assessed P5); drafting own executive brief."},{"block":"Day 4 11:15-12:15","items":"Knowledge check (42 questions, 60 minutes, closed book)."},{"block":"Day 4 12:15-13:00","items":"Lunch and prayer."},{"block":"Day 4 13:00-15:45","items":"Supervised capstone clinic 2: finalise charter, method, data access, interview plan and HP-1 date; individual tutorial with the lead facilitator."},{"block":"Day 4 15:45-16:30","items":"Close: personal commitments, course evaluation, knowledge check results and re-sit arrangements."},{"block":"Capstone weeks 1-4","items":"Data collection (option A: documents, interviews, observation; option B: data preservation, interviews, timeline). Tutor check-in call at week 3."},{"block":"Capstone weeks 5-8","items":"Analysis and draft findings; observed HP-1 delivery with accredited assessor. Tutor review of draft at week 8."},{"block":"Capstone weeks 9-12","items":"Roadmap or recommendations, executive brief, presentation to asset leadership; final submission at week 12."},{"block":"Weeks 13-14","items":"Panel viva (60 minutes) and certification decision; personal development plan agreed with sponsor."}],"modules":[{"id":"L5M1","n":1,"t":"Systemic incident investigation for human factors","mins":270,"obj":"Lead the human factors strand of a significant investigation: plan and conduct cognitive interviews, reconstruct the timeline, explain actions through local rationality, classify contributing factors with HFACS and write findings free of hindsight, outcome bias and counterfactuals.","tools":["HF investigation planning checklist (data preservation, interview order, terms of reference)","Cognitive interview guide and prompt card","Multi-row timeline template with clock-offset log","Bias-free findings checklist ('failed to' test, substitution test, peer recognition test)"],"learn":"\n<h2>What a master investigator is for</h2>\n<p>At HP-2 and HP-3 participants learned to recognise error types and the conditions that produce them. At HP-5 the job is different: you are the person the investigation lead turns to when the question is \"why did it make sense for a competent, experienced crew to do that?\" You are also the person who has to stop a draft report from concluding \"operator failed to follow procedure\" and closing out with a re-briefing. IOGP Report 621 (Demystifying human factors: building confidence in human factors investigation) makes the point plainly: investigations that stop at the individual act generate actions that do not prevent recurrence, and the human factors questions must be asked by someone with the skill and the authority to follow them into the organisation.</p>\n<div class=\"callout or\"><h4>The local rationality principle</h4><p>People do what makes sense to them at the time, given their goals, their knowledge and where their attention was focused (Dekker). The investigator's task is to reconstruct that \"inside\" view so convincingly that a peer reading the report would say \"I could have done the same\". If the report cannot explain why the action made sense, the investigation is not finished.</p></div>\n<h2>Five biases that wreck investigations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bias</th><th>What it looks like in a report</th><th>Counter-measure</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Hindsight bias</b></td><td>\"The rising H2S reading should have alerted the operator.\" Knowledge of the outcome makes the cues look obvious and the path look linear.</td><td>Reconstruct what was visible at each moment: what was on the DCS overview, what was in the radio traffic, what the operator was doing in parallel.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Outcome bias</b></td><td>The same short-cut is judged a \"violation\" after a release and \"initiative\" after a quick restart.</td><td>Ask how often the same practice occurred without consequence (work-as-done); apply the substitution test before any judgement of behaviour.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Counterfactual reasoning</b></td><td>\"Had the operator verified the isolation, the release would not have occurred.\" True, but it describes what did not happen and explains nothing.</td><td>Replace every \"failed to\", \"should have\", \"could have\" with a question: what did the person do, and why did that make sense?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Proximal focus</b></td><td>Analysis stops at the last people to touch the plant.</td><td>Follow each condition up a level: who set the staffing, approved the deferral, wrote the procedure, accepted the alarm load?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Micro-matching</b></td><td>Fragments of behaviour are compared with the procedure line by line, and every deviation becomes a finding.</td><td>Ask whether the procedure was workable as written and how the task is usually done; deviations that everyone makes are a system finding, not an individual one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Planning the human factors strand</h2>\n<p>Within the first 24 to 48 hours the human factors lead should agree with the investigation lead: who will be interviewed and in what order (people closest to the event first, while memory is fresh, but after they have had rest and welfare support); what data must be preserved (DCS historian, alarm and event journal, SIS sequence-of-events, CCTV, radio recordings, permit and isolation certificates, shift logs and handover notes, rosters and overtime records, gas detector logs, training records); and the terms of reference, which must explicitly include organisational and latent conditions, not only \"immediate and root causes\".</p>\n<h2>Interviewing: the cognitive interview</h2>\n<p>The cognitive interview (Fisher and Geiselman) was developed to improve the quantity and accuracy of witness recall without leading. Its four core techniques are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Context reinstatement:</b> ask the person to mentally return to the setting: the heat, the noise of the compressor, where they were standing, what they had just been doing, how they felt. Recall is cued by context. Where possible, interview near the location or use photographs.</li>\n<li><b>Report everything:</b> ask for every detail, even if it seems trivial or incomplete. Fragments from one witness often unlock another's account.</li>\n<li><b>Change the order:</b> after free recall, ask the person to go through events in a different order (for example backwards from the alarm). This reduces reliance on a reconstructed script.</li>\n<li><b>Change perspective:</b> ask what someone else present (the panel operator, the permit holder) would have seen or heard.</li></ol>\n<p>The enhanced cognitive interview adds rapport building, explicitly transferring control of the conversation to the witness, open questions, and allowing silences. Closed and leading questions (\"Did you check the gas test before you opened the valve?\") are held back until the end, for clarification only.</p>\n<div class=\"grid2\"><div class=\"card\"><h4>Questions that open up local rationality</h4><p>What were you trying to achieve at that point? What were you looking at? What did you expect to happen? What told you it was normal? What else was competing for your attention? Who else did you talk to? How is this usually done on nights? What would have made it easier?</p></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Questions that close it down</h4><p>Why did you not follow the procedure? Did you not see the alarm? Who told you it was safe? Were you aware of the H2S risk? Any question starting \"why did you not...\" puts the person on trial and invites defence, not recall.</p></div></div>\n<p>Practical rules: interview one person at a time, with a note taker and at most two interviewers; explain the purpose, the just culture policy and how the record will be used; allow a companion or representative if requested; never show a witness another witness's account; record \"I do not know\" as data. Interpreters should be briefed beforehand on the method; much of the Gulf workforce works in a second or third language, and nuance is easily lost.</p>\n<h2>Reconstructing the timeline</h2>\n<p>Build the sequence from objective data first (historian trends, alarm journal, SOE, radio, CCTV, permit timestamps), then lay interview accounts against it. Use a multi-row timeline: one row each for process state, alarms, control room actions, field actions, communications and the work control system. Mark clock offsets between systems: DCS, SIS and CCTV clocks are frequently several minutes apart, and an unsynchronised timeline produces false conclusions about who knew what when. Where accounts conflict with data, do not assume the person is lying: memory is reconstructive, and the conflict is itself a lead (what made the person believe the valve had closed?).</p>\n<h2>Classifying with HFACS</h2>\n<p>The Human Factors Analysis and Classification System (Shappell and Wiegmann), built on Reason's model, classifies contributing factors at four levels: <b>unsafe acts</b> (errors: skill-based, decision and perceptual; and violations: routine and exceptional), <b>preconditions for unsafe acts</b> (environmental factors, condition of operators, personnel factors such as crew resource management and fitness for duty), <b>unsafe supervision</b> (inadequate supervision, planned inappropriate operations, failure to correct a known problem, supervisory violations) and <b>organisational influences</b> (resource management, organisational climate, organisational process). <b>HFACS-OGI</b> (Theophilus and colleagues, 2017) adapts the framework for oil and gas, adding an external level for industry and regulatory influences and categories relevant to oil and gas operations. HFACS is valuable for consistency and for trending across many investigations. Its limits: it can become a box-ticking exercise, codes can be chosen to fit a preferred story, and a code is not an explanation. Use it after the local rationality analysis, not instead of it.</p>\n<h3>Sort it</h3><p>Place each finding at its HFACS level.</p>\n<div class=\"sortq\" data-sort='[[\"Operator opened the drain valve on the wrong train\",\"Unsafe acts\"],[\"Night shift operator on the 13th consecutive 12-hour shift\",\"Preconditions\"],[\"Supervisor scheduled sour draining during a crane lift over the same area\",\"Unsafe supervision\"],[\"Known faulty level gauge left in service for four months after repeated reports\",\"Unsafe supervision\"],[\"Headcount freeze removed the relief operator role\",\"Organisational influences\"],[\"Production bonus tied to zero deferred volume\",\"Organisational influences\"]]' data-opts='[\"Unsafe acts\",\"Preconditions\",\"Unsafe supervision\",\"Organisational influences\"]'></div>\n<h2>Writing findings that survive challenge</h2>\n<p>A strong finding states the condition, the mechanism and the evidence: \"The procedure for draining V-301 boot required the operator to confirm low interface level on LG-301, which had been reported faulty on four occasions since March (work orders ...). Night operators had adopted timing the drain by sound and smell, which had worked without incident. On the night, ...\" It does not say \"the operator failed to monitor the level\". Each recommendation must trace to a finding and, wherever possible, act on the condition rather than the person. Test the draft with a respected operator from another shift: if they recognise the work, it is probably right.</p>\n<div class=\"rev\"><button type=\"button\">Rewrite: \"The operator failed to perform an adequate gas test before breaking containment.\"</button><div class=\"body\"><p>\"The gas test recorded at 02:10 was taken at the permit board 30 m upwind, which is where the permit form prompts the reading to be recorded. The form does not specify the test location relative to the break point, and three of five operators interviewed described this as normal practice when the portable detector is already worn.\"</p></div></div>\n<p class=\"src\">Based on IOGP Report 621 (Demystifying human factors: building confidence in human factors investigation); Dekker, The Field Guide to Understanding Human Error; Fisher and Geiselman (1992) on the cognitive interview; Shappell and Wiegmann (2000) HFACS; Theophilus et al. (2017) HFACS-OGI; Reason (1997); HSE HSG48; Energy Institute Human Factors Briefing Notes (incident investigation).</p>","activity":{"title":"The sour drain at Wadi Saham: from draft report to systemic findings","time":"90 minutes (plus 45-minute cognitive interview practice with role-player)","scn":"<p><span class=\"tag\">Onshore sour gas plant · Gulf region</span><span class=\"tag\">Night shift · 31 °C, humid, light wind</span></p>\n<p>At Wadi Saham gas plant (fictional), a field operator draining sour water from the boot of three-phase inlet separator V-301A (approx. 4 mol% H2S in the gas phase) was found collapsed at 02:41 by a colleague. He was rescued by the standby team wearing breathing apparatus and recovered in hospital. The drain line runs to an open tundish connected to the closed drain system; the drain valve had been left open after the water phase cleared and gas blew through to the tundish.</p>\n<p><b>Facts gathered so far:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Boot interface level glass LG-301A had been reported as fouled on four work orders since March; the job was deferred twice for \"access scaffolding\".</li>\n<li>The plant was in SIMOPS: a crane lift for a turnaround pre-fabrication spool was taking place over the adjacent pipe rack, and the area authority had asked operators to keep clear of the lift zone, which included the normal standing position at the drain valve.</li>\n<li>The operator was on the 12th night of a 14-night rotation, covering for an absent colleague, and had been called to two other jobs during the drain.</li>\n<li>His personal H2S monitor alarmed at 02:33 (logged); his radio call at 02:34 was overlapped by crane communications on the same channel.</li>\n<li>The procedure says \"drain until interface is observed on LG-301A, then close drain valve\". Four of six operators interviewed said they drain \"by sound\": they listen for the change from water to gas.</li>\n<li>The DCS clock is 4 minutes ahead of the gas detector logging system.</li></ul>\n<p><b>The draft report concludes:</b> \"Root cause: the operator failed to follow the draining procedure and failed to respond to his personal gas alarm. Contributing cause: complacency. Actions: disciplinary review; re-brief all operators on the draining procedure and H2S awareness; issue a safety alert.\"</p>","qs":[{"q":"Identify every instance of hindsight, outcome bias and counterfactual language in the draft conclusion and explain why each will produce ineffective actions.","hint":"'Failed to', 'complacency', the jump from outcome to culpability, actions aimed at the person."},{"q":"Plan the cognitive interview with the injured operator: timing, setting, who is present, opening, the four core techniques applied to this event, and three questions you will avoid.","hint":"Welfare first; context reinstatement at the drain point or with photos; report everything; reverse order from the gas alarm; perspective of the crane banksman."},{"q":"Build the skeleton of a multi-row timeline for 02:00 to 02:45. What data sources do you need, and what does the 4-minute clock offset change?","hint":"Rows for process, alarms, field actions, radio, permits/SIMOPS; correct the offset before sequencing the H2S alarm against the radio call."},{"q":"Write two local rationality statements explaining why draining by sound and walking away to another job made sense to the operator.","hint":"Fouled glass, workload, crane exclusion zone, normal practice that had always worked."},{"q":"Classify the contributing factors using HFACS (or HFACS-OGI) and propose three recommendations that act on conditions rather than the individual.","hint":"Unsafe acts down to organisational influences; engineered fix for the drain, level indication, SIMOPS planning, radio channel, fatigue cover."}]},"quiz":[{"q":"Which statement best expresses the local rationality principle?","opts":["People act rationally only when well trained and rested","People do what makes sense to them at the time, given their goals, knowledge and focus of attention","Investigators should judge actions against what a rational operator would have done","Rational decisions follow procedures; irrational decisions deviate from them"],"a":1,"why":"Local rationality (Dekker) asks the investigator to reconstruct why the action made sense from the inside. Judging against an idealised rational operator is exactly the outside, hindsight view it warns against."},{"q":"In a cognitive interview, asking the witness to recall events starting from the moment the alarm sounded and working backwards is an example of:","opts":["Context reinstatement","Report everything","Change perspective","Change the order"],"a":3,"why":"Changing the order of recall reduces reliance on a reconstructed, script-based narrative and often brings out new details."},{"q":"A draft finding reads: 'Had the panel operator acknowledged the high-level alarm, the overfill would have been prevented.' What is the main problem?","opts":["It is a counterfactual that describes what did not happen and explains nothing about why","It is factually incorrect","It blames the wrong person","It should be classified under HFACS before being written"],"a":0,"why":"Counterfactuals may be true but they do not explain behaviour. The finding should describe what the operator was doing and why acknowledging that alarm did not happen (alarm load, priority, competing tasks, display)."},{"q":"In HFACS, scheduling a sour draining task at the same time as a crane lift that excludes the operator from the valve position is best classified as:","opts":["Skill-based error","Organisational climate","Planned inappropriate operations (unsafe supervision)","Adverse mental state (precondition)"],"a":2,"why":"Planned inappropriate operations covers supervisory planning that puts people in an untenable position. The organisational process that allowed the clash (SIMOPS matrix) could also be coded at the organisational level."},{"q":"The DCS historian clock is found to be 4 minutes ahead of the gas detection logging system. What is the correct action before building the timeline?","opts":["Use the DCS time as the master and ignore the gas logs","Determine and document the offset, normalise all sources to a single reference, and record the uncertainty","Average the two clocks","Rely on witness estimates of time instead"],"a":1,"why":"Unsynchronised clocks are common and can reverse the apparent order of events. Establish the offset from a common event, correct all sources and state the remaining uncertainty."},{"q":"Which is the most important limitation of HFACS as used in investigations?","opts":["It cannot be used outside aviation","It has no organisational level","It only covers violations","A code is a classification, not an explanation, and codes can be chosen to fit a preferred story"],"a":3,"why":"HFACS improves consistency and trending, but it does not explain why actions made sense. It should follow, not replace, local rationality analysis. It has been adapted for oil and gas (HFACS-OGI) and does include organisational influences."}],"nfac":7},{"id":"L5M2","n":2,"t":"Systems analysis methods: AcciMap, STAMP/CAST and FRAM","mins":210,"obj":"Apply AcciMap, CAST and FRAM at a working level to a process safety event, and choose and justify the method that fits the question, the data and the audience.","tools":["AcciMap level template (six levels, A0 format)","CAST worksheet: controller responsibilities, unsafe control actions, process model, context","FRAM function description sheet (six aspects and variability)","Method selection matrix"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1300,"exTitle":"Overfill at the Khor Taiba condensate terminal: three lenses","exTime":"100 minutes"},{"id":"L5M3","n":3,"t":"HP programme design and maturity assessment","mins":180,"obj":"Assess a site human factors programme against recognised key topics and good practice, rate its maturity on evidence, and design a governed, resourced roadmap integrated with the process safety management system.","tools":["HF programme gap assessment protocol (11 topics, evidence grid)","Five-level maturity scale with rating rules","Roadmap template (three phases, owners, resources, dependencies)","RBPS and SEMS integration map"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"Gap assessment and roadmap for Ras Bandar NGL plant","exTime":"75 minutes"},{"id":"L5M4","n":4,"t":"Facilitation and coaching mastery","mins":240,"obj":"Deliver HP-1 to HP-4 and facilitate learning teams to the pathway delivery standard, applying adult learning principles, managing group dynamics and coaching leaders through difficult conversations about failure.","tools":["Pathway delivery standard and observation scoresheet","Learning team facilitator guide (discovery, soak time, improvement)","GROW coaching card for HP conversations","Micro-teach feedback form"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1000,"exTitle":"Three facilitation moments at Jebel Nur","exTime":"70 minutes (plus 70-minute filmed micro-teach)"},{"id":"L5M5","n":5,"t":"Advanced HRA and human barrier management","mins":210,"obj":"Evaluate whether human actions in bow-ties and LOPA are valid barriers, specify them with barrier performance standards, design verification, treat alarm response as a barrier, and critique human reliability assessments for misuse.","tools":["Human barrier validation checklist (effective, independent, auditable; detect-decide-act; time)","Barrier performance standard template for human actions","Human barrier verification plan and drill timing record","HRA critique checklist"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"Gas blow-by at Hamra Field: is the operator a barrier?","exTime":"75 minutes"},{"id":"L5M6","n":6,"t":"Organisational factors and resilience engineering","mins":210,"obj":"Evaluate an organisation's resilience potentials, high reliability characteristics, staffing arrangements, organisational changes and fatigue risk using structured methods, and recommend defensible decisions.","tools":["Resilience Analysis Grid question set for operating assets","Staffing assessment scenario walk-through worksheet","Organisational change HF screening checklist","FRMS design checklist (API RP 755 elements)"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":1100,"exTitle":"Control room consolidation at Umm Lasafa","exTime":"75 minutes"},{"id":"L5M7","n":7,"t":"Influencing and change leadership","mins":180,"obj":"Build a credible business case for the HP programme, communicate it to executives, diagnose and manage resistance with ADKAR, lead change with Kotter's stages, and embed the programme so that it outlives its champions.","tools":["HP business case template (risk, cost of status quo, programme cost, benefits, measures)","One-page executive brief template (pyramid structure)","ADKAR diagnostic grid for HP roll-out","Programme sustainment checklist"],"locked":true,"quiz":[],"nq":6,"words":900,"exTitle":"Ten minutes with the new general manager at Barr Al Qasr","exTime":"70 minutes (including executive briefing simulation)"}],"assessment":{"summary":"Four parts. (1) A closed-book knowledge check of 42 multiple-choice questions (six per module) on Day 4. (2) In-course practical assessments observed and scored by the lead facilitator against the rubric below: a cognitive interview (M1), a method-selection justification (M2), a barrier performance standard (M5), a staffing recommendation (M6) and an executive briefing (M7); the M4 micro-teach is formative. (3) The 3-month workplace capstone, including an observed HP-1 delivery scored by an accredited assessor. (4) A 60-minute panel viva at the end of the capstone, with the lead assessor, a second HP-5 or chartered human factors assessor and a senior line leader from the participant's organisation. Certification is subject to the recertification policy below.","pass":"80% or more on the knowledge check (34 of 42) AND 'Competent' on every in-course practical criterion AND 'Competent' or better on every capstone rubric criterion, including the observed HP-1 facilitation AND a viva outcome of 'Competent'. Distinction is awarded where at least five capstone criteria and the viva are marked Distinction with none below Competent. One re-sit of the knowledge check is allowed. An in-course practical marked Not Yet Competent may be re-attempted once, either before the end of Day 4 or as an equivalent task submitted with the capstone. A capstone criterion marked Not Yet Competent may be resubmitted once within 6 weeks of feedback; a failed observed facilitation may be repeated once with a different group.","practical":null},"capstone":{"title":"Workplace capstone: programme gap assessment and roadmap, or systemic investigation, plus an observed HP-1","deliverables":["Project charter (2 pages) signed by the sponsor before work starts: scope, option, method, access and timeline.","Option A: gap assessment report (20 to 30 pages plus evidence appendix) with maturity ratings and evidence, MAH-linked gap prioritisation, deep-dive, and a 2- to 3-year roadmap with governance, resourcing, indicators and sustainment plan. OR Option B: systemic investigation report (20 to 30 pages plus appendices) with interview plan and anonymised notes, multi-source timeline, local rationality analysis, HFACS or HFACS-OGI coding, systems method output, and traceable recommendations.","One-page executive brief for the asset leadership team, in pyramid structure, with the decisions requested.","Observed HP-1 delivery record: session plan, local scenarios, assessor scoresheet, participant evaluation summary and a one-page reflection.","Viva presentation (15 minutes) and a personal development plan for the first year as an HP lead."],"brief":null}});
